You can create a simple HTML webpage and use JavaScript to replace words using a table. Here's a sample code to demonstrate this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Word Replacer</title>
    <script defer>
      document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
        const wordMap = {
          "original": "replaced",
          "old": "new",
          "apple": "orange",
        };

        function replaceWords() {
          const contentElement = document.getElementById("content");

          const originalText = contentElement.innerHTML;
          const words = originalText.split(/\b/); // split into words
          const replacedText = words
            .map((word) => (wordMap[word] !== undefined ? wordMap[word] : word))
            .join("");

          contentElement.innerHTML = replacedText;
        }

        document
          .getElementById("replace-button")
          .addEventListener("click", replaceWords);
      });
    </script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="content">
      This is the original content with some old words like apple.
    </div>
    <button id="replace-button">Replace words</button>
  </body>
</html>
```

In this example, we have an HTML document with a `div` element containing some text (ID "content") and a button (ID "replace-button") to trigger the word replacing. The JavaScript code listens for a click event on the button and then runs the `replaceWords` function to replace the words in the content `div`.

The word replacement table is defined in the `wordMap` object, where the keys are the original words and the values are the replaced words. To add more words or modify existing replacements, simply update this object.

To automatically replace words in the `<body>` element without needing to click a button, you just need to call the `replaceWords` function in the `DOMContentLoaded` event listener. Here's the updated `replaceWords.js` file:

```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
  const wordMap = {
    "original": "replaced",
    "old": "new",
    "apple": "orange",
  };

  function replaceWords() {
    const contentElement = document.body;

    const originalText = contentElement.innerHTML;
    const words = originalText.split(/\b/); // split into words
    const replacedText = words
      .map((word) => (wordMap[word] !== undefined ? wordMap[word] : word))
      .join("");

    contentElement.innerHTML = replacedText;
  }

  // Automatically replace words on page load
  replaceWords();
});
```

I made two changes to the previous code:

1. I changed the `contentElement` to `document.body` to target the entire body content.
2. I removed the event listener for the button and added a call to `replaceWords()` at the end of the `DOMContentLoaded` callback, which will automatically run the function when the page loads.

You can also remove the button from the HTML file because it's no longer needed:

```html
