Creating a complete Android Flashcard app is beyond the scope of this platform. However, here is a simple outline of how you can create a basic Flashcard app in Kotlin.

1. Set up a new Android project in Android Studio with Kotlin support.

2. Create a `Flashcard` data class to represent flashcards. This class should have two member properties: `front` and `back`, both of type `String`.

```kotlin
data class Flashcard(val front: String, val back: String)
```

3. Add a ListView or RecyclerView to display the list of flashcards. You can design the layout for each item in the list to show a small preview of the flashcard's content.

4. Create data for the flashcards, such as a list of `Flashcard` objects. You can hard-code this list or load it from a file or database.

5. Create an adapter for the ListView/RecyclerView to display the list of flashcards using the data created in the previous step. If you're using ListView, extend `ArrayAdapter<Flashcard>`. If you're using RecyclerView, extend `RecyclerView.Adapter<MyAdapter.MyViewHolder>` and create the ViewHolder class inside your adapter.

6. Handle click events for each element in the list. When a user clicks on an item, the app should display the full information for the selected flashcard.

7. Create a new activity or fragment to display the selected flashcard. This view should display the front and show the back when a user clicks on the card. You can use a CardView to create a simple flashcard layout and handle click events to flip the card.

Here's an example of how the adapter might look like for a RecyclerView:

```kotlin
class FlashcardAdapter(private val flashcards: List<Flashcard>, private val onItemClick: (Flashcard) -> Unit) :
    RecyclerView.Adapter<FlashcardAdapter.FlashcardViewHolder>() {

    override fun onCreateViewHolder(parent: ViewGroup, viewType: Int): FlashcardViewHolder {
        val view = LayoutInflater.from(parent.context).inflate(R.layout.flashcard_item, parent, false)
        return FlashcardViewHolder(view)
    }

    override fun getItemCount(): Int {
        return flashcards.size
    }

    override fun onBindViewHolder(holder: FlashcardViewHolder, position: Int) {
        holder.bind(flashcards[position], onItemClick)
    }

    class FlashcardViewHolder(itemView: View) : RecyclerView.ViewHolder(itemView) {
        private val frontTextView: TextView = itemView.findViewById(R.id.flashcard_front)

        fun bind(flashcard: Flashcard, onItemClick: (Flashcard) -> Unit) {
            frontTextView.text = flashcard.front
            itemView.setOnClickListener { onItemClick(flashcard) }
        }
    }
}
```

Creating a full-featured Flashcard app will require proper UI/UX design, storing data, and handling user input. Additionally, you may want to add more features like creating and saving new flashcards or using a built-in dictionary to help with language learning.

```java
import java.text.DecimalFormat;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class GasStation {
    private final int numPumps;
    private final Pump[] pumps;

    public GasStation(int numPumps) {
        this.numPumps = numPumps;
        this.pumps = new Pump[numPumps];
        for (int i = 0; i < numPumps; i++) {
            pumps[i] = new Pump(i + 1);
        }
    }

    public void serveCustomer(Customer customer) {
        System.out.println("Customer " + customer.id + " paying $" + customer.payment + " for gas.");
        Pump selectedPump = findAvailablePump();
        if (selectedPump == null) {
            System.out.println("Error: No available pump for customer " + customer.id + ".");
            return;
        }

        selectedPump.activate(customer);
    }

    private Pump findAvailablePump() {
        for (Pump pump : pumps) {
            if (!pump.busy) {
                return pump;
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        GasStation gasStation = new GasStation(3);
        Customer[] customers = new Customer[]{
                new Customer(1, 10),
                new Customer(2, 5)
        };
        for (Customer customer : customers) {
            gasStation.serveCustomer(customer);
        }
    }

    private static class Pump {
        private final int id;
        private boolean busy;

        public Pump(int id) {
            this.id = id;
            this.busy = false;
        }

        public void activate(Customer customer) {
            this.busy = true;
            System.out.println("Pump " + id + " activated for customer " + customer.id + ".");
            double providedGas = customer.payment / 2;
            DecimalFormat decimalFormat = new DecimalFormat("#.##");
            System.out.println("Pump " + id + " delivers $" + decimalFormat.format(providedGas) + " of gas to customer " + customer.id + ".");
            if (Math.abs(providedGas - customer.payment) > 0.01) {
                System.out.println("Error: Customer " + customer.id + " not satisfied. Incorrect amount of gas delivered.");
            }
            this.busy = false;
        }
    }

    private static class Customer {
        private final int id;
        private final double payment;

        public Customer(int id, double payment) {
            this.id = id;
            this.payment = payment;
        }
    }
}
```

To create a base UI library for MAUI projects, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new .NET MAUI library project
First, create a new .NET MAUI library project that will serve as the base UI library.

- Open a terminal or command prompt and navigate to a directory where your projects are stored.
- Install the templates for .NET MAUI if you haven't already done so by running `dotnet new --install Microsoft.Maui.Templates`.
- Execute the following command to create a new .NET MAUI library project:
```sh
dotnet new maui-lib --name YourBaseUILibrary
```
Replace `YourBaseUILibrary` with the desired name for your project. This will create a new folder with the same name and the necessary files for a .NET MAUI library.

2. Create your custom components and styles.
   Inside your `YourBaseUILibrary` project, create custom UI components and styles. For example, if you want to create a custom button, you can create a new C# class that inherits the MAUI Button class and adds your custom functionality/styles.

- Add a new folder named `Controls`
- Inside `Controls` folder, create a new C# file named `CustomButton.cs`
- Add this code to `CustomButton.cs`:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Maui.Controls;

namespace YourBaseUILibrary.Controls
{
    public class CustomButton : Button
    {
        // Now you can add your custom functionality and styles to this class.
        public CustomButton()
        {
            // For example, set a default background color:
            BackgroundColor = Color.Red;
        }
    }
}
```

3. Build your library project.
   Build your `YourBaseUILibrary` project to create the library output.

```sh
cd YourBaseUILibrary
dotnet build
```

4. Reference `YourBaseUILibrary` in your MAUI project.
- Open the .csproj file of your main MAUI project in a text editor.
- Add a `ProjectReference` to the `ItemGroup` section like this:

```xml
<ItemGroup>
    <!-- ... -->
    <ProjectReference Include="path/to/YourBaseUILibrary/YourBaseUILibrary.csproj" />
</ItemGroup>
```

5. Use your custom components in your MAUI app.
In your main MAUI project, you can now import and use the custom components you created in `YourBaseUILibrary`. For example, to use the `CustomButton` component:

```csharp
using YourBaseUILibrary.Controls;
using Microsoft.Maui.Controls;

namespace MyApp
{
    public class MainPage : ContentPage
    {
        public MainPage()
        {
            var myCustomButton = new CustomButton()
            {
                Text = "Click me!",
                HorizontalOptions = LayoutOptions.Center,
                VerticalOptions = LayoutOptions.Center
            };

            Content = myCustomButton;
        }
    }
}
```

Now, when you build and run your main MAUI app, you will see your custom button from the base UI library displayed on the screen.

Remember to keep your base UI library up-to-date and distribute it to other projects when you add or update components.

