To read frequency with an ATmega16 microcontroller in C, you can leverage its 16-bit Timer/Counter1, which includes the Input Capture functionality. I'll provide you with an example demonstrating how to use Timer/Counter1 to measure frequency.

For the example, let's assume you have a frequency signal connected to the Input Capture Pin, ICP1 (PD4 or PORTD.4, Pin 18 on ATmega16).

**Note**: For a better understanding of the example, consult the ATmega16 datasheet to reference the registers and descriptions, which is available at: https://www.ece.uc.edu/~omearac/5320/AVR%20Documents/AtMega16_Datasheets.pdf

1. Configure Timer/Counter1 registers and initialize the Input Capture function of ATmega16.

```c
#define F_CPU 8000000UL // Define your CPU frequency, e.g., 8MHz
#include <avr/io.h>
#include <avr/interrupt.h>

volatile uint32_t captured_frequency;

void timer1_init()
{
    TCCR1A = 0; // Set Timer/Counter1 Control Register A to 0
    TCCR1B |= (1 << CS11) | (1 << ICES1); // Set the prescaler to 8, and capture on rising edge
    TIMSK |= (1 << TICIE1); // Enable Input Capture Interrupt for Timer/Counter1
    sei(); // Enable global interrupts
}
```

2. Write the ISR for the Input Capture Interrupt.

```c
uint16_t icp_previous = 0;
uint16_t icp_current = 0;

ISR(TIMER1_CAPT_vect)
{
    icp_previous = icp_current;
    icp_current = ICR1;

    captured_frequency = F_CPU / 8UL / (icp_current - icp_previous);
}
```

3. Initialize the timer and set up the USART for debugging, then check the `captured_frequency` variable in the `main` function loop. Here's an example setup:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define BAUD_RATE 9600
#define UBRR_VALUE 51 // (F_CPU/16/BAUD_RATE-1)

void uart_init()
{
    UBRRH = (uint8_t)(UBRR_VALUE >> 8);
    UBRRL = (uint8_t)UBRR_VALUE;
    UCSRB |= (1 << RXEN) | (1 << TXEN);
    UCSRC |= (1 << URSEL) | (1 << UCSZ1) | (1 << UCSZ0); // Set 8-bit character size
}

int uart_putchar(char c, FILE *stream)
{
    if (c == '\n') uart_putchar('\r', stream);
    loop_until_bit_is_set(UCSRA, UDRE);
    UDR = c;
    return 0;
}

FILE uart_output = FDEV_SETUP_STREAM(uart_putchar, NULL, _FDEV_SETUP_WRITE);

int main(void)
{
    timer1_init();
    uart_init();

    stdout = &uart_output;

    while (1)
    {
        printf("Frequency: %lu Hz\n", captured_frequency);
        _delay_ms(1000); // Use util/delay.h (_delay_ms()) or create your own delay routine
    }
}
```

Now, the example code initializes Timer/Counter1 on an ATmega16 to capture frequency connected at the Input Capture pin and sends the captured frequency over UART for debugging. You can modify the example according to your specific requirements.

To measure the frequency of a humidity sensor with ATmega16 using Timer/Counter0 and Timer/Counter2, you can use Timer0 as a counter to count the number of pulses and Timer2 as a timer to measure a specific duration. After the duration, the Timer2 overflow interrupt will calculate the frequency based on the number of pulses counted.

1. First, define and include the necessary headers:

```c
#define F_CPU 8000000UL // Define your CPU frequency, e.g., 8MHz
#include <avr/io.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <util/delay.h>
#include <avr/interrupt.h>
```

2. Configure Timer/Counter0 as a counter connected to the humidity sensor:

```c
void timer0_init()
{
    // Normal Mode, external clock on T0 (PD4)
    TCCR0 = (1 << CS02) | (1 << CS01) | (1 << CS00);
    TCNT0 = 0; // Initialize counter value to zero
}
```

3. Configure Timer/Counter2 as a timer to measure the specific duration with an overflow interrupt:

```c
void timer2_init()
{
    // Normal Mode, Prescaler = 256
    TCCR2 = (1 << CS22) | (1 << CS21);
    TIMSK |= (1 << TOIE2); // Enable Timer/Counter2 Overflow Interrupt
    sei();
}
```

4. Write the ISR for Timer/Counter2 Overflow Interrupt to calculate the frequency:

```c
volatile uint32_t pulse_count = 0;
volatile uint32_t humidity_sensor_frequency = 0;

ISR(TIMER2_OVF_vect)
{
    pulse_count = TCNT0; // Read pulse count from Timer/Counter0
    TCNT0 = 0;           // Reset Timer/Counter0

    // Calculate frequency
    humidity_sensor_frequency = pulse_count; // Calculate the frequency based on the number of pulses counted
}
```

5. Initialize the timers and use the `humidity_sensor_frequency` variable in the `main` function loop:

```c
int main(void)
{
    timer0_init();
    timer2_init();

    while (1)
    {
        // Use humidity_sensor_frequency variable for your application, e.g., display or calculations
        _delay_ms(1000);
    }
}
```

Now, Timer/Counter0 counts the pulses from the humidity sensor, whereas Timer/Counter2 generates an overflow interrupt every ~1s (assuming an 8 MHz clock and a prescaler of 256). You can change the Timer2 prescaler and the overflow interrupt duration to get a different sampling time.

To calibrate a 10-bit ADC's values to read resistance, you'll typically use a voltage divider circuit with a known reference resistor and the resistor whose value you'd like to measure. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to do this:

1. Set up a voltage divider circuit:
Connect the ground (GND) to one end of the reference resistor (R_ref), and to one end of the resistor you'd like to measure (R_x). Connect the ADC's input (ADC_pin) to the junction between R_ref and R_x. And finally, connect the power supply (Vcc) to the other end of R_x.

2. Initialize the 10-bit ADC:

```c
void adc_init()
{
    ADMUX = (1 << REFS0); // AVcc as the reference voltage
    ADCSRA = (1 << ADEN) | (1 << ADPS2) | (1 << ADPS1) | (1 << ADPS0); // Enable ADC and set the prescaler to 128
}
```

3. Write a function to read ADC values:

```c
uint16_t read_adc(uint8_t adc_channel)
{
    ADMUX = (ADMUX & 0xF0) | (adc_channel & 0x0F); // Select ADC channel
    ADCSRA |= (1 << ADSC); // Start conversion
    while (ADCSRA & (1 << ADSC)); // Wait for the conversion to complete
    return ADC; // Return ADC value
}
```

4. Calibrate the ADC values to read the resistance:

```c
float get_resistance(uint8_t adc_channel, float reference_resistor_value)
{
    uint16_t adc_value = read_adc(adc_channel);
    float voltage_at_junction = ((float)adc_value / 1023.0) * 5.0; // Convert ADC value to voltage (assuming 5V reference)
    float resistance = (voltage_at_junction * reference_resistor_value) / (5.0 - voltage_at_junction); // Calculate unknown resistance using voltage divider formula
    return resistance;
}
```

5. Combine everything and use the calibration function in your main loop:

```c
#include <avr/io.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>

#define ADC_CHANNEL 0 // Define your ADC channel, e.g., ADC0
#define REFERENCE_RESISTOR_VALUE 1000.0f // Set the value of the reference resistor here (in Ohms)

int main(void)
{
    adc_init();

    while (1)
    {
        float resistance = get_resistance(ADC_CHANNEL, REFERENCE_RESISTOR_VALUE);

        // Use the resistance value for your application
    }
}
```

In this example, we initialize the 10-bit ADC, read ADC values, and then use a calibration function to calculate the resistance based on a known reference resistor value. The unknown resistance is calculated using a voltage divider formula. Adjust the ADC channel, reference voltage, and reference resistor depending on your specific setup.

