```

2. Configure and initialize the logger
In your `main.rs` or another suitable module, initialize the `env_logger` and configure it, if needed:

```rust
use log::{info, warn, error};

fn main() {
    // Initialize the logger and set the level of logging messages to display
    env_logger::Builder::new()
        .filter_level(log::LevelFilter::Info)
        .init();

    // Your actual code
    app();
}

fn app() {
    info!("This is an info log message");
    warn!("This is a warning log message");
    error!("This is an error log message");

    // Other application code...
}
```
Here, we set the log level filter to `Info`, which means it will display log messages at the levels: `Info`, `Warn`, `Error`. You can adjust it according to your needs.

3. Using log macros
