For this example, let's create a simple Vue.js application with Vue Router and transitions between different routes. We will create a simple Home and About page and animate the transition between these two routes. Make sure you have Node.js and npm installed on your system to follow this example.

1. First, install the Vue CLI globally:

```bash
npm install -g @vue/cli
```

2. Create a new Vue.js project:

```bash
vue create vue-router-transition-example
```

Select the default preset and go to the newly created project directory:

```bash
cd vue-router-transition-example
```

3. Add Vue Router to the project:

```bash
vue add router
```

Choose "Yes" when asked if you want to use history mode.

4. Now, let's edit the `src/views/Home.vue` file to change its content. Replace its content with the following code:

```html
<template>
  <div class="home">
    <h1>Home</h1>
    <p>This is the home page</p>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  name: "Home",
};
</script>
```

5. Similarly, edit the `src/views/About.vue` file:

```html
<template>
  <div class="about">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>This is the about page</p>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  name: "About",
};
</script>
```

6. Next, edit the router by opening `src/router/index.js` file:

```javascript
import { createRouter, createWebHistory } from "vue-router";
import Home from "../views/Home.vue";
import About from "../views/About.vue";

const routes = [
  {
    path: "/",
    name: "Home",
    component: Home,
  },
  {
    path: "/about",
    name: "About",
    component: About,
  },
];

const router = createRouter({
  history: createWebHistory(process.env.BASE_URL),
  routes,
});

export default router;
```

7. Now, let's add a transition to the route views. Edit the `src/App.vue` file:

```html
<template>
  <div id="app">
    <div id="nav">
      <router-link to="/">Home</router-link> |
      <router-link to="/about">About</router-link>
    </div>
    <transition name="fade">
      <router-view/>
    </transition>
  </div>
</template>

<style>
#app {
  font-family: Avenir, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
  text-align: center;
}

#nav {
  padding: 30px;
}

#nav a {
  font-weight: bold;
  color: #2c3e50;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.fade-enter-active,
.fade-leave-active {
  transition: opacity 0.5s;
}

.fade-enter, .fade-leave-to {
  opacity: 0;
}
</style>
```

In this step, we've wrapped the `router-view` component with a `transition` component and added CSS for the fade transition effect.

8. Now, your project is set up and ready to experience route transitions. Start the development server by running:

```bash
npm run serve
```

9. Open your browser and go to `http://localhost:8080`. You should see the Home and About links in the navbar, and when you navigate between these routes, you'll experience a simple fade transition.

