The noise floor is generated by adding a normal (Gaussian) random number to each frequency bin with the given standard deviation. The signals are generated by calculating their complex spectrum (which has a Gaussian shape centered at the signal frequency) and then converting it to a power spectrum (which is the magnitude squared of the complex spectrum). The power spectrum is added to the total spectrum for all frequency bins that fall within the signal bandwidth.

Note that this implementation assumes that the signals are narrowband, i.e., their bandwidth is much smaller than the sample rate. If the signals are wideband, the Gaussian shape of the complex spectrum will overlap with neighboring frequency bins, which may cause artifacts in the total spectrum. To handle wideband signals, a more sophisticated method such as FFT-based filtering could be used.

