In Jetpack Compose, you can easily update the UI in real-time by using state management. Here's an example of how you can create a composable function that updates the UI in real-time:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyLayout() {
    var value by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }

    Column(
        modifier = Modifier
            .fillMaxWidth()
            .padding(16.dp),
        horizontalAlignment = Alignment.CenterHorizontally
    ) {
        Text(text = "Value: $value", fontSize = 24.sp)
        Spacer(modifier = Modifier.height(16.dp))
        Button(onClick = { value++ }) {
            Text(text = "Increment")
        }
    }

    LaunchedEffect(value) {
        while (true) {
            delay(1000L)
            value += 1 // increment the value every second
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we're using the `mutableStateOf` function to create a state variable called `value` that defaults to 0. We then display the value in a `Text` view and provide a button that increments the value when clicked.

We also use the `LaunchedEffect` composable function to create a coroutine that runs in the background and updates the `value` variable every second. Whenever `value` changes, the composable function will recompose, and the UI will update in real-time.

You can use this function in your `setContent` function to display the layout:

```kotlin
setContent {
    MyLayout()
}
```

This will display a layout with a `Text` view that shows the current value and a `Button` that increments the value. The value will be incremented every second, and the UI will update in real-time.

