In a React application, you can create an Axios HTTP client as follows:

Start by installing axios as a dependency:

```bash
npm install axios
```

or

```bash
yarn add axios
```

Now create a file named `httpClient.js` in the `src` folder of your React application:

```javascript
// src/httpClient.js
import axios from 'axios';

const httpClient = axios.create({
  baseURL: 'https://your-base-url.com/api/',  // Replace with your base API URL
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json'
  }
});

httpClient.interceptors.request.use((config) => {
  // Add logic for request manipulation here if necessary
  // For example, add an authentication token to the headers
  const token = localStorage.getItem('token');
  if (token) {
    config.headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${token}`;
  }
  return config;
}, (error) => {
  return Promise.reject(error);
});

httpClient.interceptors.response.use((response) => {
  // Process the response if necessary
  return response;
}, (error) => {
  if (error.response && error.response.status === 401) {
    // Handle unauthorized errors, such as needing to log in again
    // For example, clear user data and redirect to login page
  }
  return Promise.reject(error);
});

export default httpClient;
```

This creates an Axios instance, which sets the base URL and content type of your requests. It also applies request and response interceptors for custom logic like adding authentication tokens or handling errors.

Now, you can use this `httpClient` in your React components or services:

```javascript
// src/exampleService.js
import httpClient from './httpClient';

export const fetchData = async () => {
  try {
    const response = await httpClient.get('data-endpoint');
    return response.data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching data:', error.message);
    throw error;
  }
};
```

```javascript
// src/App.js
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { fetchData } from './exampleService';

function App() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const loadData = async () => {
      try {
        const fetchedData = await fetchData();
        setData(fetchedData);
      } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error loading data:', error.message);
      }
    };
    loadData();
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Data</h1>
      {!data && <p>Loading...</p>}
      {data && <pre>{JSON.stringify(data, null, 2)}</pre>}
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

In the examples above, we create an `exampleService` using the `httpClient` to fetch data from the API, and load the data in a React component.

