The most commonly used and fastest data type in Java to implement the weights of a deep learning network is a multi-dimensional array of float or double primitives, depending on the required precision. The float data type is often more memory-efficient and has faster computation, which is important for large-scale deep learning networks.

Here's an example of initializing a simple deep learning network with three layers and random weights using float data type:

```java
import java.util.Random;

public class SimpleDeepLearningNetwork {
    private float[][] layer1Weights;
    private float[][] layer2Weights;
    private float[][] layer3Weights;

    public SimpleDeepLearningNetwork(int inputSize, int hiddenSize1, int hiddenSize2, int outputSize) {
        // Initialize weights with random values
        Random rand = new Random();

        // Initialize layer 1 weights
        layer1Weights = new float[inputSize][hiddenSize1];
        for (int i = 0; i < inputSize; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < hiddenSize1; j++) {
                layer1Weights[i][j] = rand.nextFloat();
            }
        }

        // Initialize layer 2 weights
        layer2Weights = new float[hiddenSize1][hiddenSize2];
        for (int i = 0; i < hiddenSize1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < hiddenSize2; j++) {
                layer2Weights[i][j] = rand.nextFloat();
            }
        }

        // Initialize layer 3 weights
        layer3Weights = new float[hiddenSize2][outputSize];
        for (int i = 0; i < hiddenSize2; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < outputSize; j++) {
                layer3Weights[i][j] = rand.nextFloat();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

You can change the float data type to double if you require higher precision, but it will consume more memory and may be slower for some operations.

