The `sessionCreated()` method is called when a new session is created, and the `sessionDestroyed()` method is called when a session is destroyed (when the session ends/times out or when it is invalidated). If you are seeing these methods being called three times, it could be due to one of the following reasons:

1. Multiple sessions are being created: Make sure only one session is being created per user. If you have any code that creates a new session, check if a session already exists and reuse it instead of creating a new one.

```java
HttpSession session = req.getSession(false);
if (session == null) {
    session = req.getSession();
}
```

2. Your application creates multiple sessions or logs the user in multiple times: Ensure that your application is not logging the user in more than once or creating redundant sessions by analyzing the application flow and session management code.

3. Browser/client behavior: If your users open your application in multiple tabs or windows, it's possible that the browser is creating a new session for each tab or window. You can use cookies to synchronize sessions across multiple tabs or windows.

4. Misconfigured or aggressive session timeout settings: If the session timeout is too short or the session is invalidated too early, it may cause the session to be destroyed and a new one to be created multiple times.

To debug the issue, you can add log statements in the `sessionCreated()` and `sessionDestroyed()` methods to gather more information about when and why these methods are being called. This will help you identify the source of the issue and address it.

