  if (svgRef.current) {
    console.log("SVG class name:", svgRef.current.className.baseVal);
  }
};
```

Note that for this example to work, your SVG element should have a `class` attribute defined, e.g.

```javascript
<svg ref={svgRef} className="my-svg" width="100" height="100">
```

Now you can call the `getSvgClassName` function to log the className of the SVG at any time. However, make sure that the function is called when the component is mounted or when you interact with the component, since the ref's `current` property may be `null` if your element has not yet been mounted.

Here's the complete code:

```javascript
import React, { useRef } from 'react';

const SvgComponent = () => {
  const svgRef = useRef(null);

  const getSvgClassName = () => {
    if (svgRef.current) {
      console.log("SVG class name:", svgRef.current.className.baseVal);
    }
