Here's an example C# script for Unity that generates a uniformly scattered set of points on a signed distance field (SDF) defined implicity surface (in this case, a sphere):

```c#
using UnityEngine;

[ExecuteAlways]
public class SDFScatterPoints : MonoBehaviour
{
    public float radius = 1f;
    public int numPoints = 1000;
    public GameObject pointPrefab;

    private void Start()
    {
        ScatterPoints();
    }

    private void ScatterPoints()
    {
        // Create an array to store the points
        Vector3[] points = new Vector3[numPoints];

        // Generate random positions
        for (int i = 0; i < numPoints; i++)
        {
            bool pointValid = false;

            while (!pointValid)
            {
                // Generate a random position within the bounding cube of the sphere
                Vector3 point = new Vector3(Random.Range(-radius, radius), Random.Range(-radius, radius), Random.Range(-radius, radius));

                // Check if the point is inside the sphere
                if (SignedDistance(point) < 0f)
                {
                    points[i] = point;
                    pointValid = true;
                }
            }
        }

        // Instantiate point objects and position them at the generated positions
        for (int i = 0; i < numPoints; i++)
        {
            Instantiate(pointPrefab, points[i], Quaternion.identity, transform);
        }
    }

    // Signed distance function for a sphere centered at the origin with radius 'radius'
    private float SignedDistance(Vector3 point)
    {
        return Vector3.Distance(point, Vector3.zero) - radius;
    }

#if UNITY_EDITOR
    // Visualize the sphere in the Scene view
    private void OnDrawGizmos()
    {
        Gizmos.color = Color.blue;
        Gizmos.DrawWireSphere(transform.position, radius);
    }
#endif
}
```

This script generates a set of `numPoints` random positions within the bounding cube of the sphere, and checks if each point is inside or outside of the sphere using a signed distance function. If the point is inside the sphere, it is added to an array of valid points.

After all `numPoints` are generated, the script instantiates `pointPrefab` objects and positions them at the generated positions. These points can be any kind of GameObject, but the script assumes that they have a default scale of (1, 1, 1).

To use this script, attach it to an empty GameObject in the scene and specify the desired parameters in the inspector (radius, numPoints, and pointPrefab). The script will automatically generate and position the point objects in the scene.

