If you set `XACT_ABORT` to ON at the beginning of your script and want to revert it to the previous setting when your script finishes, you can add `SET XACT_ABORT OFF;` at the end of your script. However, in most cases, it's sufficient to set it to ON if required, and you don't need to revert it at the end, as the setting will only apply to your current session, and other sessions will have their own separate settings. If you're concerned about preserving the original setting, you can store the current setting in a variable and restore it at the end of the script:

```sql
DECLARE @original_xact_abort_setting AS VARCHAR(3);
SELECT @original_xact_abort_setting = CONVERT(VARCHAR(3), @@OPTIONS & 16384);
SET XACT_ABORT ON;

-- Your queries and transactions go here

IF @original_xact_abort_setting = '0'
    SET XACT_ABORT OFF;
```

As for using TRY/CATCH blocks outside a transaction, you can use them to handle errors and roll back changes without using `XACT_ABORT`. You can start a transaction inside the TRY block and use the CATCH block to handle errors and roll back the transaction if needed. Here's an example:

```sql
BEGIN TRY
    BEGIN TRANSACTION;

    -- Your SELECT and DELETE queries go here

    COMMIT TRANSACTION;
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
    IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0
        ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;

    -- Handle the error (e.g., log the error, display a message)
END CATCH;
```

With this approach, you don't need to set `XACT_ABORT`. If an error occurs during the transaction, the script will automatically jump to the CATCH block, and any active transactions will be rolled back. So, using TRY/CATCH is a good alternative to using `XACT_ABORT`. It ultimately depends on your preference and the structure of your script.

