Unity Economy Services is not a built-in feature in Unity. Instead, you can implement an economy system by creating a script that manages the player's in-game currency and purchases. Here's a simple example of how it can be done:

1. Create a new C# script, name it `EconomyManager`, and open it in your preferred code editor.

2. Inside the script, create a variable to store the player's current money and a method to check if the player has enough money for a purchase. You might also create methods to add or subtract money when appropriate. The script may look similar to this:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class EconomyManager : MonoBehaviour
{
    public int currentPlayerMoney = 1000; // Starting player's money

    // Returns true if the player has enough money to make the purchase
    public bool CanAfford(int cost)
    {
        return currentPlayerMoney >= cost;
    }

    // Deduct the cost from the player's money
    public void MakePurchase(int cost)
    {
        if (CanAfford(cost))
        {
            currentPlayerMoney -= cost;
        }
        else
        {
            Debug.LogError("Insufficient funds for purchase");
        }
    }

    // Add funds to the player's money
    public void AddMoney(int amount)
    {
        currentPlayerMoney += amount;
    }  
}
```

