`GridLayoutManager` is a part of Android's `Leanback` support library that's designed for TV apps. It's a layout manager mainly for displaying items in a grid format with flexible customizations options.

`spanCount` is an attribute in `GridLayoutManager`, representing the number of columns in the grid. By setting this value, you can control the number of columns displayed in the `RecyclerView`. For example, if you set `spanCount` to 3, your grid would have three columns.

To set `spanCount` in `GridLayoutManager`, you need to initialize the layout manager and attach it to RecyclerView.

For example, here's how you can implement it in your app:

1. Include the `Leanback` support library in your project's `app/build.gradle` file (the version might change; you can check the latest version at [mvnrepository](https://mvnrepository.com/artifact/androidx.leanback/leanback)):

```gradle
dependencies {
    implementation 'androidx.leanback:leanback:1.2.0'
}
```

2. In your activity main layout file, add the `RecyclerView`:

```xml
<androidx.recyclerview.widget.RecyclerView
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:id="@+id/recyclerView"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:scrollbars="vertical" />
```

3. Create a GridLayoutManger, set the `spanCount`, and assign it to the RecyclerView in your Activity:

```java
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import androidx.recyclerview.widget.GridLayoutManager;
import androidx.recyclerview.widget.RecyclerView;

public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {

    private RecyclerView recyclerView;
    private int spanCount = 3;

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);

        recyclerView = findViewById(R.id.recyclerView);

        // Initialize and set the GridLayoutManager
        GridLayoutManager layoutManager = new GridLayoutManager(this, spanCount);
        recyclerView.setLayoutManager(layoutManager);

        // Set your RecyclerView adapter here
    }
}
```

That's it! Now your RecyclerView will display items in a grid format with the number of columns specified by the `spanCount`. Note that while we used Leanback's GridLayoutManager here, you could also achieve this layout using the default androidx GridLayoutManager in a similar way.

