Here's a Java program that uses dynamic programming to solve the wildcard pattern matching problem with "?" and "*":

```java
public class WildcardMatching {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "abcdef";
        String pattern = "a?c*f";

        System.out.println(isMatch(str, pattern));
    }

    public static boolean isMatch(String str, String pattern) {
        int m = str.length();
        int n = pattern.length();

        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[m + 1][n + 1];

        // Empty pattern can match with empty string
        dp[0][0] = true;

        // Only '*' can match with the empty string
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            if (pattern.charAt(j - 1) == '*') {
                dp[0][j] = dp[0][j - 1];
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {

                // Two cases when current character is '?', or current characters are the same
                if (pattern.charAt(j - 1) == '?' || str.charAt(i - 1) == pattern.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                }

                // When current character is '*', it can either act as an empty character, or as the same character
                else if (pattern.charAt(j - 1) == '*') {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j] || dp[i][j - 1];
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[m][n];
    }
}
```

This program defines a method `isMatch` that takes two parameters `str` (the input string) and `pattern` (the wildcard pattern). This method checks whether the input string `str` matches the given wildcard pattern considering '?' as any single character and '*' as a sequence of characters.

To test the code, simply change the "str" and "pattern" variables in the main method with your desired values.

