To create the back-end server, you'll use Express.js, which is a minimal and flexible Node.js web application framework. Follow these steps to set up the Express.js server:

1. Install dependencies:

Navigate to your project folder in the terminal, then run the following command to install Express.js, along with other essential dependencies:

```sh
npm install express mongoose cors dotenv
```

- `express`: The main web application framework
- `mongoose`: Object Data Modeling (ODM) library for MongoDB and Node.js
- `cors`: Middleware to handle Cross-Origin Resource Sharing, enabling communication between front-end and back-end servers
- `dotenv`: Utility to load environment variables from a .env file

2. Create the main server file:

Inside the `backend` folder, create a new file named `server.js`. This file will hold the main server configurations and setup.

3. Set up the Express.js server:

Open the `server.js` file and add the following code to import the necessary dependencies, set up the Express.js server, and create a simple hello world route:

```js
const express = require("express");
const mongoose = require("mongoose");
const cors = require("cors");
const dotenv = require("dotenv");

dotenv.config();

//Initialize express app
const app = express();

//Middleware
app.use(express.json());
app.use(cors());

//Simple hello world route
app.get("/", (req, res) => {
  res.send("Hello world!");
});

//Connect to MongoDB
mongoose
  .connect(process.env.MONGO_URI, {
    useNewUrlParser: true,
    useUnifiedTopology: true,
  })
  .then(() => console.log("Connected to MongoDB"))
  .catch((error) => console.error("Unable to connect to MongoDB:", error));

//Start the server
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 5000;
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server is running on port ${PORT}`);
});
```

