Creating a Discord app with FTP capabilities requires some understanding of coding, specifically in JavaScript or Python, since these are popular languages for Discord bots. If you're familiar with either language, follow the steps below to set up a basic Discord app with FTP functionality. We'll use JavaScript for this example, but the process would be similar for Python.

1. Set up a Discord bot
- Create an account on Discord if you haven't already.
- Go to the Discord Developer Portal: https://discord.com/developers/applications
- Click "New Application", enter a name for your app and click "Create".
- Go to the "Bot" tab and click "Add Bot", then confirm the prompt.
- Copy the "TOKEN" value (you'll need this later).

2. Install the required tools and libraries
- Install Node.js (https://nodejs.org/) if you haven't already.
- In your project folder, run `npm init` to set up a new npm project.
- Install the discord.js library by running `npm install discord.js`.
- Install the FTP library (e.g., basic-ftp) by running `npm install basic-ftp`.

3. Write the Discord bot code
- Create a `index.js` file in your project folder and add the following code:

```javascript
const Discord = require('discord.js');
const ftp = require('basic-ftp');

const client = new Discord.Client();
const FTP_USER = 'your_ftp_username';
const FTP_PASSWORD = 'your_ftp_password';
const FTP_HOST = 'your_ftp_host';

client.on('ready', () => {
    console.log(`Logged in as ${client.user.tag}!`);
});

client.on('message', async message => {
    if (message.content.startsWith('!upload')) {
        // Replace 'path/to/local/file.txt' with your local file path 
        // and 'remote/file/path.txt' with the desired path on your FTP server
        await uploadFile('path/to/local/file.txt', 'remote/file/path.txt', message);
    }
    // Add more commands as needed
});

async function uploadFile(localPath, remotePath, message) {
    const client = new ftp.Client();

    try {
        await client.access({
            host: FTP_HOST,
            user: FTP_USER,
            password: FTP_PASSWORD
        });

        await client.uploadFrom(localPath, remotePath);
        message.reply(`uploaded ${localPath} to ${remotePath}`);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
        message.reply(`error uploading file: ${error}`);
    } finally {
        client.close();
    }
}

// Replace 'your_bot_token' with the Discord bot token you copied earlier
client.login('your_bot_token');
```

4. Run the Discord bot
- Start your bot by running `node index.js` in your project folder.
- Invite your bot to your Discord server (you can find an invite link in the "OAuth2" tab of your bot settings in the Discord Developer Portal).
- Once the bot is in your server, you can use the `!upload` command to upload a file to your FTP server.

Remember to replace the placeholder values (FTP_USER, FTP_PASSWORD, FTP_HOST, and your_bot_token) with your own credentials.

Keep in mind that this is a basic example to upload a single file, and you'll have to add more functionality (e.g., changing existing files, specifying files to upload) according to your specific requirements. You can refer to the documentation of the basic-ftp library for more information on how to work with files on an FTP server: https://github.com/patrickjuchli/basic-ftp

Here is a complete, working, and well-commented code sample to authenticate with an Azure tenancy using Microsoft.Identity.Client and a registered app secret.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Identity.Client;
using Microsoft.Graph;
using Microsoft.Graph.Auth;

namespace AzureADAppSecretAuthSample
{
    class Program
    {
        // Replace these with your own tenant ID, client ID, and client secret.
        private const string TenantId = "your-tenant-id";
        private const string ClientId = "your-client-id";
        private const string ClientSecret = "your-client-secret";

        private static readonly string[] Scopes = { "https://graph.microsoft.com/.default" };

        static async Task Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Create a PublicClientApplicationBuilder with your TenantId and ClientId
            IConfidentialClientApplication app = ConfidentialClientApplicationBuilder
                .Create(ClientId)
                .WithClientSecret(ClientSecret)
                .WithAuthority(new Uri($"https://login.microsoftonline.com/{TenantId}"))
                .Build();

            // Acquire token using the app secret
            AuthenticationResult result = await app.AcquireTokenForClient(Scopes).ExecuteAsync();

            // Initialize the GraphServiceClient with the access token.
            GraphServiceClient graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(new DelegateAuthenticationProvider((requestMessage) =>
            {
                requestMessage.Headers.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", result.AccessToken);
                return Task.FromResult(0);
            }));

            // Call Microsoft Graph and display user info.
            var me = await graphClient.Me.Request().GetAsync();
            Console.WriteLine($"Hello, {me.DisplayName}!");

            // List the user's calendar events
            var events = await graphClient.Me.Events.Request().GetAsync();
            Console.WriteLine("Your upcoming events:");
            foreach (var eventObj in events)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"{eventObj.Subject} - {eventObj.Start.DateTime}");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Don't forget to replace the TenantId, ClientId, and ClientSecret values with the appropriate values from your Azure AD App registration.

