Drawing a 3D tetrahedron in Rust implies you would need to use a graphics library such as `glium` or `wgpu`. Here, I will provide you a basic example focused on rendering a 3D tetrahedron using the `glium` library.

Make sure you have added the `glium` crate to your `Cargo.toml` file as follows:

```toml
[dependencies]
glium = "0.29"
```

Here is a simple Rust code that draws a tetrahedron using glium:

```rust
#[macro_use]
extern crate glium;

mod vertex_helper {
    use glium::implement_vertex;

    #[derive(Clone, Copy)]
    pub struct Vertex {
        pub position: [f32; 3],
    }

    implement_vertex!(Vertex, position);
}

use vertex_helper::Vertex;
use glium::{glutin, Surface};

fn main() {
    let mut events_loop = glutin::event_loop::EventLoop::new();
    let wb = glutin::window::WindowBuilder::new().with_title("Tetrahedron");
    let cb = glutin::ContextBuilder::new();
    let display = glium::Display::new(wb, cb, &events_loop).unwrap();

    let vertex1 = Vertex { position: [-1.0, -1.0, 0.0] };
    let vertex2 = Vertex { position: [1.0, -1.0, 0.0] };
    let vertex3 = Vertex { position: [0.0, 1.0, 0.0] };
    let vertex4 = Vertex { position: [0.0, 0.0, 1.0] };

    let shape = vec![
        vertex1, vertex2, vertex3, vertex4, // Declare tetrahedron vertices
    ];

    let vertex_buffer = glium::VertexBuffer::new(&display, &shape).unwrap();
    let indices = glium::index::NoIndices(glium::index::PrimitiveType::TrianglesList);

    let vertex_shader_src = r#"
        #version 140

        in vec3 position;

        void main() {
            gl_Position = vec4(position, 1.0);
        }
    "#;

    let fragment_shader_src = r#"
        #version 140

        out vec4 color;

        void main() {
            color = vec4(1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
        }
    "#;

    let program = glium::Program::from_source(
        &display,
        vertex_shader_src,
        fragment_shader_src,
        None,
    ).unwrap();

    events_loop.run(move |event, _, control_flow| {
        let next_frame_time = std::time::Instant::now() + std::time::Duration::from_nanos(16_666_667);
        *control_flow = glutin::event_loop::ControlFlow::WaitUntil(next_frame_time);

        match event {
            glutin::event::Event::WindowEvent { event, .. } => match event {
                glutin::event::WindowEvent::CloseRequested => {
                    *control_flow = glutin::event_loop::ControlFlow::Exit;
                    return;
                }
                _ => (),
            },
            _ => (),
        }

        let mut target = display.draw();
        target.clear_color(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
        target.draw(
            &vertex_buffer,
            &indices,
            &program,
            &glium::uniform! {},
            &Default::default(),
        ).unwrap();
        target.finish().unwrap();
    });
}
```

This example creates a window with a red triangle resembling the base of the tetrahedron. You may also want to consider using a 3D rendering library like `three` or `amethyst` if you are looking for a more complex 3D rendering pipeline.

To authenticate with an Azure tenancy using Microsoft.Identity and a registered app secret, you can follow these steps:

1. Register your application in the Azure Active Directory (AAD) portal, and get its application ID (clientId) and client secret.

2. Install the required NuGet packages:
- Microsoft.Identity.Client
- Microsoft.Graph

3. Add the following class (AuthenticationProvider.cs) to your project:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Graph;
using Microsoft.Identity.Client;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace MyOutlookPlugin
{
    public class AuthenticationProvider : IAuthenticationProvider
    {
        private readonly string _clientId;
        private readonly string _clientSecret;
        private readonly string _tenantId;
        private readonly string[] _scopes;

        public AuthenticationProvider(string clientId, string tenantId, string clientSecret, string[] scopes)
        {
            _clientId = clientId;
            _tenantId = tenantId;
            _clientSecret = clientSecret;
            _scopes = scopes;
        }

        public async Task<string> GetAccessTokenAsync()
        {
            var app = ConfidentialClientApplicationBuilder
                .Create(_clientId)
                .WithTenantId(_tenantId)
                .WithClientSecret(_clientSecret)
                .Build();

            var result = await app.AcquireTokenForClient(_scopes).ExecuteAsync();

            return result.AccessToken;
        }

        public async Task AuthenticateRequestAsync(HttpRequestMessage requestMessage)
        {
            var accessToken = await GetAccessTokenAsync();
            requestMessage.Headers.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", accessToken);
        }
    }
}
```

4. Update your App.config or App.xaml.cs file to have your app settings:

```xml
<appSettings>
  <add key="ClientId" value="your_client_id"/>
  <add key="TenantId" value="your_tenant_id"/>
  <add key="ClientSecret" value="your_client_secret"/>
  <add key="Scopes" value="https://graph.microsoft.com/.default"/>
</appSettings>
```

5. Instantiate the `AuthenticationProvider` and Microsoft Graph `GraphServiceClient` in your main class:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Graph;
using System.Configuration;

namespace MyOutlookPlugin
{
    public class MainClass
    {
        private static GraphServiceClient _graphClient;

        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Read settings from the config file
            var clientId = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["ClientId"];
            var tenantId = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["TenantId"];
            var clientSecret = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["ClientSecret"];
            var scopes = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["Scopes"].Split(';');

            var authenticationProvider = new AuthenticationProvider(clientId, tenantId, clientSecret, scopes);
            _graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(authenticationProvider);

            // Now you can use the _graphClient object to interact with the Microsoft Graph API.
        }
    }
}
```

Now you must have a complete and working integration with Microsoft Graph using your registered app secret. The `GraphServiceClient` object can be used to access different services such as users, mails, calendars, etc. For more information, check out the [Microsoft Graph documentation](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/overview).

To request the appropriate scopes for editing a calendar, you can update the "Scopes" setting in your App.config file as follows:

```xml
<appSettings>
  <add key="ClientId" value="your_client_id"/>
  <add key="TenantId" value="your_tenant_id"/>
  <add key="ClientSecret" value="your_client_secret"/>
  <add key="Scopes" value="https://graph.microsoft.com/Calendars.ReadWrite"/>
</appSettings>
```

Note: Make sure to grant the required Calendar permissions to your app in the Azure Active Directory (AAD) portal. These permissions include:

- Calendars.ReadWrite: Allows the app to create, read, update, and delete events in all calendars that the user has access to.
- Calendars.ReadWrite.Shared: Allows the app to create, read, update, and delete events in all calendars the user has access to, and any shared with the user.

Now, you can use the Microsoft.Graph.Calendar API to interact with calendars. For example, you can create an event in the user's calendar as shown below:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Graph;
using System;
using System.Configuration;

namespace MyOutlookPlugin
{
    public class MainClass
    {
        private static GraphServiceClient _graphClient;

        static async void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Read settings from the config file
            var clientId = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["ClientId"];
            var tenantId = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["TenantId"];
            var clientSecret = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["ClientSecret"];
            var scopes = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["Scopes"].Split(';');

            var authenticationProvider = new AuthenticationProvider(clientId, tenantId, clientSecret, scopes);
            _graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(authenticationProvider);

            // Now you can use the _graphClient object to interact with the Microsoft Graph API.
            await CreateEvent();
        }

        private static async Task CreateEvent()
        {
            var newEvent = new Event
            {
                Subject = "Test Event",
                Start = new DateTimeTimeZone
                {
                    DateTime = DateTime.UtcNow.AddHours(1).ToString("o"),
                    TimeZone = "UTC"
                },
                End = new DateTimeTimeZone
                {
                    DateTime = DateTime.UtcNow.AddHours(2).ToString("o"),
                    TimeZone = "UTC"
                }
            };

            await _graphClient.Me.Events.Request().AddAsync(newEvent);
            Console.WriteLine("Event created.");
        }
    }
}
```

Now you have a complete and working integration with Microsoft Graph using the appropriate scopes for editing a calendar. The `GraphServiceClient` object can be used to access different calendar services such as creating, reading, updating, and deleting events. For more information, check out the [Microsoft Graph documentation](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/overview).

