Now, you have an interface `URagnaBaseAbilityType`, and any class that wants to implement this interface can do so by inheriting from `IRagnaBaseAbilityType`, as shown in the `AMyClass` example.

Remember that interfaces in Unreal Engine are different from regular classes. They define a contract or a set of functions that any class implementing the interface must provide. UObject interfaces should inherit from `UInterface` and use the `I` prefix (e.g., `IMyInterface`, `IRagnaBaseAbilityType`).

To create a base UObject class that implements your interface, follow these steps:

1. Define your interface (if you haven't done this already). The following example demonstrates how to create a UObject interface called `IMyInterface`:

```cpp
// MyInterface.h
#pragma once

#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "MyInterface.generated.h"

UINTERFACE(Blueprintable)
class UMyInterface : public UInterface
{
    GENERATED_BODY()
};

class IMyInterface
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    UFUNCTION(BlueprintCallable, Category="MyInterface")
    virtual void MyInterfaceFunction();
};
```

2. Create a base UObject class that implements the interface:

```cpp
// MyBaseObject.h
#pragma once

#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "MyInterface.h"
#include "MyBaseObject.generated.h"

UCLASS(Blueprintable)
class UMyBaseObject : public UObject, public IMyInterface
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    virtual void MyInterfaceFunction() override;
};
```

```cpp
// MyBaseObject.cpp
#include "MyBaseObject.h"

void UMyBaseObject::MyInterfaceFunction()
{
    // Your implementation here
}
```

Now you've created a base UObject class called `UMyBaseObject` that implements the `IMyInterface`. Any class that inherits from `UMyBaseObject` will already implement the `IMyInterface` and can override the `MyInterfaceFunction()` as needed.

To derive a class from `UMyBaseObject`, use the following syntax:

```cpp
// MyDerivedObject.h
#pragma once

#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "MyBaseObject.h"
#include "MyDerivedObject.generated.h"

UCLASS(Blueprintable)
class UMyDerivedObject : public UMyBaseObject
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    virtual void MyInterfaceFunction() override;
};
```

```cpp
// MyDerivedObject.cpp
#include "MyDerivedObject.h"

void UMyDerivedObject::MyInterfaceFunction()
{
    // Your implementation here
}
```

In this example, `UMyDerivedObject` is a class that inherits from `UMyBaseObject` and can provide its own implementation of the `MyInterfaceFunction()`.

Any class derived from `UMyBaseObject` will implement the `IMyInterface`, and you can use this base object for TSubclassOf or any other reference.

