To build a display queue based on addChild architecture and use renderQuad to draw the elements, we need to create a simple scene graph system that helps manage the display objects and their transformations. Here's a basic implementation:

```javascript
class DisplayObject {
  constructor() {
    this.children = [];
    this.parent = null;
    this.x = 0;
    this.y = 0;
    this.width = 0;
    this.height = 0;
  }

  addChild(child) {
    if (child.parent) {
      child.parent.removeChild(child);
    }
    child.parent = this;
    this.children.push(child);
  }

  removeChild(child) {
    const index = this.children.indexOf(child);
    if (index !== -1) {
      child.parent = null;
      this.children.splice(index, 1);
    }
  }

  updateTransform() {
    this.transform = {
      x: this.parent ? this.parent.transform.x + this.x : this.x,
      y: this.parent ? this.parent.transform.y + this.y : this.y,
    };

    for (const child of this.children) {
      child.updateTransform();
    }
  }
}

class Quad extends DisplayObject {
  constructor(width, height) {
    super();
    this.width = width;
    this.height = height;
  }

  render() {
    renderQuad(this.transform.x, this.transform.y, this.width, this.height);

    for (const child of this.children) {
      child.render();
    }
  }
}

function renderQuad(x, y, width, height) {
  console.log(`Rendering quad at (${x}, ${y}), width: ${width}, height: ${height}`);
  // Draw the quad using your preferred rendering method (e.g., Canvas, WebGL, etc.)
}

// Example usage:

const root = new DisplayObject();
const quad1 = new Quad(50, 50);
const quad2 = new Quad(30, 30);

quad1.x = 10;
quad1.y = 20;
quad2.x = 25;
quad2.y = 35;

root.addChild(quad1);
quad1.addChild(quad2);

root.updateTransform();

quad1.render();  // Will also render quad2 as its child
```

This code snippet creates a simple scene graph system with `DisplayObject` instances that have transformation properties (x, y, width, height), a way to add and remove children using `addChild` and `removeChild`, and an updating transform based on parent objects. The `Quad` class extends `DisplayObject` and is responsible for rendering using the `renderQuad` function.

The example usage at the bottom demonstrates adding two quads to the display hierarchy and performing transformation calculations using the `updateTransform` method. When `quad1.render()` is called, it also renders `quad2` as it is a child of `quad1`.

