This example demonstrates PHP session management with a simple login form. The example consists of three separate files: `login.html`, `auth.php`, and `welcome.php`. 

### login.html
This file contains the HTML template for the login form that the user fills in.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Login Form</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Login</h1>
    <form action="auth.php" method="POST">
        <label for="username">Username:</label>
        <input type="text" name="username" id="username" required>
        <br>

        <label for="password">Password:</label>
        <input type="password" name="password" id="password" required>
        <br>

        <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

### auth.php
This file handles the authentication process by checking the submitted form data against stored values. You can replace the hard-coded values with those retrieved from a database. If the authentication is successful, the file initializes a session and redirects the user to the `welcome.php` page. Otherwise, it redirects back to the login page.

```php
<?php
// Check if form is submitted
if ($_SERVER["REQUEST_METHOD"] == "POST") {
    // Replace with your own values, or query a database to retrieve these
    $stored_username = 'admin';
    $stored_password = 'password';

    // Retrieve submitted values
    $username = $_POST['username'];
    $password = $_POST['password'];

    // Check if submitted values match stored values
    if ($username == $stored_username && $password == $stored_password) {
        // Start a new session
        session_start();

        // Assign authenticated user's username to the session
        $_SESSION['username'] = $username;

        // Redirect to the welcome page
        header('Location: welcome.php');
        exit();
    } else {
        // Authentication failed, redirect back to login page
        header('Location: login.html');
        exit();
    }
}
?>
```

### welcome.php
This file consists of an HTML template that displays a welcome message, including the user's username. If the user is not authenticated, the file redirects them back to the login page.

```php
<?php
session_start();

// Check if user is authenticated
if (!isset($_SESSION['username'])) {
    // Redirect to login page
    header('Location: login.html');
    exit();
}
?>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Welcome</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome, <?php echo htmlspecialchars($_SESSION['username']); ?>!</h1>
    <p>You are now logged in.</p>

    <form action="logout.php" method="POST">
        <button type="submit">Logout</button>
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

### logout.php
This file destroys the session and effectively logs the user out, redirecting them back to the login page.

```php
<?php
session_start();
session_unset();
session_destroy();
header('Location: login.html');
exit();
?>
```

You can now run this example on a PHP-enabled web server to see a simple login form with session management in action.

