Here is the Java code to receive IMAP mail and store subject "number" and message example "12345678" in a Hashtable.

```java
import java.util.*;
import javax.mail.*;
import javax.mail.internet.*;

public class ImapMail {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        String host = "imap.gmail.com";
        String username = "example@gmail.com";
        String password = "password";

        // create properties object for the IMAP connection
        Properties properties = new Properties();
        properties.setProperty("mail.store.protocol", "imap");
        properties.setProperty("mail.imap.host", host);
        properties.setProperty("mail.imap.port", "993");
        properties.setProperty("mail.imap.ssl.enable", "true");

        // create session with the credentials
        Session session = Session.getInstance(properties, new Authenticator() {
            protected PasswordAuthentication getPasswordAuthentication() {
                return new PasswordAuthentication(username, password);
            }
        });

        // create the IMAP store and connect to the server
        Store store = session.getStore("imap");
        store.connect(host, username, password);

        // create the inbox folder and open it in read-only mode
        Folder inbox = store.getFolder("INBOX");
        inbox.open(Folder.READ_ONLY);

        // get the messages in the inbox folder
        Message[] messages = inbox.getMessages();

        // create a Hashtable to store the subject and message body
        Hashtable<String, String> mailHash = new Hashtable<String, String>();

        // iterate through the messages and add the subject and message to the Hashtable
        for (int i = 0; i < messages.length; i++) {
            String subject = messages[i].getSubject();
            String body = getTextFromMessage(messages[i]);

            // check if the subject contains the word "number"
            if (subject.toLowerCase().contains("number")) {
                mailHash.put(subject, body);
            }
        }

        // print out the Hashtable
        System.out.println(mailHash);

        // close the mailbox and store
        inbox.close(false);
        store.close();
    }

    // helper function to get the text from a Message object
    private static String getTextFromMessage(Message message) throws Exception {
        String result = "";
        if (message.isMimeType("text/plain")) {
            result = message.getContent().toString();
        } else if (message.isMimeType("multipart/*")) {
            MimeMultipart multipart = (MimeMultipart) message.getContent();
            result = getTextFromMimeMultipart(multipart);
        }
        return result;
    }

    // helper function to get the text from a MimeMultipart object
    private static String getTextFromMimeMultipart(MimeMultipart multipart) throws Exception {
        String result = "";
        int count = multipart.getCount();
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
            BodyPart bodyPart = multipart.getBodyPart(i);
            if (bodyPart.isMimeType("text/plain")) {
                result = result + "\n" + bodyPart.getContent();
                break;
            } else if (bodyPart.isMimeType("multipart/*")) {
                result = result + getTextFromMimeMultipart((MimeMultipart) bodyPart.getContent());
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

}
```

This code connects to the IMAP server using SSL and retrieves the messages in the INBOX folder. It then iterates through the messages and checks if the subject contains the word "number". If it does, it adds the subject and message body to a Hashtable. The Hashtable is then printed out. This code also includes helper functions to extract the text from a Message and MimeMultipart object.

