You can set a 302 status code with a redirect URL in Node.js using the `response.redirect()` method. Here's an example:

```javascript
const http = require('http');

const server = http.createServer((request, response) => {
  if (request.url === '/redirect') {
    response.writeHead(302, { 'Location': 'http://www.example.com' });
    response.end();
  } else {
    response.writeHead(200);
    response.end('Hello, world!');
  }
});

server.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server listening on port 3000');
});
```

In this example, we create an HTTP server that listens on port 3000. When a request is made to the server with a URL of `/redirect`, the server sends a 302 status code with a `Location` header of `http://www.example.com`, telling the browser to redirect to that URL.

If any other URL is requested, the server sends a 200 status code with the response of "Hello, world!".

Note that the `response.redirect()` method is a shorthand for setting a 302 status code and a `Location` header. If you prefer, you can set these headers manually with the `response.writeHead()` method, as shown in the example above.

