```

In this example, we are loading three.js from the CDN, but you can also download it and include it as a local file.

Now, let's create the index.js file where we will define our panorama spheres.

```javascript
// Define the scene and camera
const scene = new THREE.Scene();
const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera( 75, window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight, 0.1, 1000 );

// Define the renderer
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer();
renderer.setSize( window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight );
document.body.appendChild( renderer.domElement );

// Define the panorama spheres
const panorama1 = createPanorama('panorama1.jpg', Math.PI / 2, 0, 0); // front
const panorama2 = createPanorama('panorama2.jpg', -Math.PI / 2, 0, 0); // back
const panorama3 = createPanorama('panorama3.jpg', Math.PI, 0, 0); // left
const panorama4 = createPanorama('panorama4.jpg', 0, 0, 0); // center
const panorama5 = createPanorama('panorama5.jpg', 0, 0, -Math.PI / 2); // top
const panorama6 = createPanorama('panorama6.jpg', 0, 0, Math.PI / 2); // bottom

// Add the panorama spheres to the scene
scene.add( panorama1 );
scene.add( panorama2 );
scene.add( panorama3 );
scene.add( panorama4 );
scene.add( panorama5 );
scene.add( panorama6 );

// Set the camera position
camera.position.set(0, 0, 0.1);

// Render the scene
function animate() {
  requestAnimationFrame( animate );
  renderer.render( scene, camera );
}
animate();

// Create a panorama sphere
function createPanorama( texture, x, y, z ) {
  const loader = new THREE.TextureLoader();
  const geometry = new THREE.SphereGeometry( 50, 60, 40 );
  const material = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ map: loader.load( texture ) });
  material.side = THREE.BackSide;
  const sphere = new THREE.Mesh( geometry, material );
  sphere.position.set( x, y, z );
  return sphere;
}
```

In this code, we first define the scene, camera, and renderer. Then, we create six panorama spheres using the `createPanorama` function, and add them to the scene. Finally, we set the camera position and start the animation loop to render the scene.

The `createPanorama` function loads a texture and creates a sphere geometry with that texture mapped onto it. We set the `side` property of the material to `THREE.BackSide` so that the texture is visible from inside the sphere.

Now, we can add interactivity to navigate between the panorama spheres. Let's add event listeners to the canvas element to listen for mouse movement and clicks:

```javascript
let previousX = 0;

renderer.domElement.addEventListener('mousedown', function (event) {
  event.preventDefault();
  previousX = event.clientX;
});

renderer.domElement.addEventListener('mousemove', function (event) {
  event.preventDefault();
  if (event.buttons === 1) {
    const delta = event.clientX - previousX;
    camera.rotation.y += delta * 0.01;
    previousX = event.clientX;
  }
});

renderer.domElement.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
  event.preventDefault();
  const raycaster = new THREE.Raycaster();
  const mouse = new THREE.Vector2();
  mouse.x = ( event.clientX / window.innerWidth ) * 2 - 1;
  mouse.y = - ( event.clientY / window.innerHeight ) * 2 + 1;
  raycaster.setFromCamera( mouse, camera );
  const intersects = raycaster.intersectObjects( scene.children );
  if ( intersects.length > 0 && intersects[0].object instanceof THREE.Mesh ) {
    camera.lookAt( intersects[0].point );
  }
});
```

We store the previous mouse position when the mouse button is pressed, and rotate the camera around the y-axis when the mouse is moved while the button is pressed.

We also add a click event listener to detect when the user clicks on a panorama sphere. We use a raycaster to detect the object clicked, and set the camera lookAt property to point to the intersection point.

With these additions, we have a functional virtual tour in three.js where the user can navigate between different panorama spheres by clicking on them. Feel free to add more interactivity and styling to make your virtual tour even more engaging.

