To use an encoder with an Arduino, you'll need to connect the encoder to the Arduino board and write a code to read the encoder's outputs. Here's an example of how to do this using a rotary encoder:

First, connect the encoder pins to the Arduino board:
- GND pin to Arduino GND
- VCC pin to Arduino 5V
- DT pin to Arduino pin 2
- CLK pin to Arduino pin 3
- SW pin to Arduino pin 4 (optional, if your encoder has a push-button switch)

Next, upload the following code to your Arduino board:

```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// Encoder pin definitions
const int DT = 2;
const int CLK = 3;
const int SW = 4; // Optional, for push-button switch

// Variable to store the encoder position
volatile long encoderPos = 0;

// Variables to store the encoder pin states
boolean clkLastState, dtLastState;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);

  // Set encoder pins as inputs
  pinMode(DT, INPUT);
  pinMode(CLK, INPUT);

  // Optional: Set switch pin as input with internal pull-up resistor
  pinMode(SW, INPUT_PULLUP);

  // Read and store the initial pin states
  clkLastState = digitalRead(CLK);
  dtLastState = digitalRead(DT);

  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(CLK), updateEncoder, CHANGE);
}

void loop() {
  // Print the encoder position
  Serial.println(encoderPos);

  // Optional: Check for switch press
  if (digitalRead(SW) == LOW) {
    Serial.println("Switch pressed");
    delay(200); // Debounce delay
  }

  delay(100);
}

void updateEncoder() {
  boolean clkState = digitalRead(CLK);
  boolean dtState = digitalRead(DT);

  if (clkLastState != clkState) {
    if (dtLastState != dtState) {
      encoderPos++; // Clockwise rotation
    } else {
      encoderPos--; // Counterclockwise rotation
    }
  }

  clkLastState = clkState;
  dtLastState = dtState;
}
```

This code sets the encoder pins as inputs, reads their states, and updates the `encoderPos` variable based on the changes in those states. If your encoder also has a push-button switch, you can read its state by checking the value of the `SW` pin. The encoder position is printed to the Serial Monitor, and you can observe the changes as you rotate the encoder.

Don't forget to adjust the pin numbers according to your setup, and make sure the Arduino is correctly connected to the encoder.

In Axum, a WebSocket-based application can handle different types of text messages sent by users for different actions such as joining a room, setting a username, and sending a message.

First, let's define the message structure for these different actions:

```json
{
  "action": "SET_USERNAME",
  "username": "JohnDoe"
}

{
  "action": "JOIN_ROOM",
  "room": "General"
}

{
  "action": "SEND_MESSAGE",
  "room": "General",
  "message": "Hello, everyone!"
}
```

Here's how we can handle these actions using Axum WebSockets:

1. Create a WebSocket server.

```rust
use axum::prelude::*;
use axum::handler::ws::tungstenite;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let app = route("/", get(|| async { "Hello, Axum WebSocket!" })).route( "/websocket", get(tungstenite(ws_handler)));

    let addr = SocketAddr::from(([127, 0, 0, 1], 3000));
    log::info!("listening on {}", addr);

    axum::Server::bind(&addr)
        .serve(app.into_make_service())
        .await
        .unwrap();
}
```

2. Implement the `ws_handler` function to handle WebSocket connections and messages.

```rust
use axum::handler::ws::{tungstenite::WebSocket, Message};
use serde_json::Value;

async fn ws_handler(ws: WebSocket) {
    let (mut tx, mut rx) = ws.split();

    while let Some(msg) = rx.try_next().await.unwrap() {
        if let Message::Text(text) = msg {
            if let Ok(value) = serde_json::from_str::<Value>(&text) {
                if let Some(action) = value.get("action") {
                    match action.as_str() {
                        Some("SET_USERNAME") => set_username(&mut tx, &value).await,
                        Some("JOIN_ROOM") => join_room(&mut tx, &value).await,
                        Some("SEND_MESSAGE") => send_message(&mut tx, &value).await,
                        _ => continue,
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

3. Implement the corresponding functions for each action.

```rust
use tokio_tungstenite::tungstenite::protocol::frame::coding::CloseCode;

async fn set_username(tx: &mut SplitSink<WebSocket, Message>, value: &Value) {
    let username = value.get("username").and_then(Value::as_str);

    match username {
        Some(username) => {
            let res = format!("Username set to {}", username);
            tx.send(Message::Text(res)).await.unwrap();
        }
        None => {
            tx.send(Message::Close(Some(CloseCode::InvalidPayload.into())))
                .await
                .unwrap();
        }
    }
}

async fn join_room(tx: &mut SplitSink<WebSocket, Message>, value: &Value) {
    let room = value.get("room").and_then(Value::as_str);

    match room {
        Some(room_name) => {
            let res = format!("Joined room {}", room_name);
            tx.send(Message::Text(res)).await.unwrap();
        }
        None => {
            tx.send(Message::Close(Some(CloseCode::InvalidPayload.into())))
                .await
                .unwrap();
        }
    }
}

async fn send_message(tx: &mut SplitSink<WebSocket, Message>, value: &Value) {
    let room = value.get("room").and_then(Value::as_str);
    let msg_content = value.get("message").and_then(Value::as_str);

    if let Some(room_name) = room {
        if let Some(msg) = msg_content {
            let res = format!("Message sent to room {}: {}", room_name, msg);
            tx.send(Message::Text(res)).await.unwrap();
        } else {
            tx.send(Message::Close(Some(CloseCode::InvalidPayload.into())))
                .await
                .unwrap();
        }
    } else {
        tx.send(Message::Close(Some(CloseCode::InvalidPayload.into())))
            .await
            .unwrap();
    }
}
```

Now, when you run the server, it will handle different types of text messages and perform the respective actions, such as setting a user's name, joining a room, and sending messages. Note that this example is quite simplified for demonstration purposes, and you'll need a more sophisticated implementation for a production chat application.

