The code you provided is a Java program implementing the hailstone sequence. It takes an integer "n" as input, and generates the hailstone sequence for that number. The hailstone sequence is defined as follows:

1. If "n" is even, divide it by 2
2. If "n" is odd, multiply it by 3 and add 1
3. Repeat the process until the sequence reaches the number 1.

However, there is an issue in the given code: it will cause a stack overflow error for large input numbers due to recursion. To fix this, you can use a loop instead, as shown below:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Main obj = new Main();
        Scanner s = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
        int input = s.nextInt();
        obj.hailstone(input);
    }

    void hailstone(int n) {
        System.out.print(n + " ");
        while (n != 1) {
            if (n % 2 == 0) {
                n = n / 2;
            } else {
                n = n * 3 + 1;
            }
            System.out.print(n + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

This code will handle large input numbers as well without causing a stack overflow error. It also takes input from the user and prints the hailstone sequence for that number.

