A simple way to implement a priority queue in C++ is to use the priority_queue container adapter from the Standard Library. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>

using namespace std;

int main() {
    priority_queue<int> pq;

    // Insert elements into the priority queue
    pq.push(30);
    pq.push(10);
    pq.push(20);
    pq.push(40);
    pq.push(50);

    // Print the top element of the priority queue
    cout << "Top element: " << pq.top() << endl;

    // Remove and print elements from the priority_queue
    cout << "Elements in priority order: ";
    while(!pq.empty()) {
        cout << pq.top() << " ";
        pq.pop();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code snippet demonstrates the use of the priority_queue container adapter from the Standard Library in C++. It allows you to insert elements into a priority queue, access the top element, and remove it. By default, the elements are sorted in descending order, so higher priority items come first.

If you want to use a custom comparison function or different types, you can pass the appropriate parameters when creating the priority_queue object. For example, If you want your priority queue to be sorted in ascending order:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <vector>
#include <functional>

using namespace std;

int main() {
    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> pq;

    // Insert elements into the priority queue
    pq.push(30);
    pq.push(10);
    pq.push(20);
    pq.push(40);
    pq.push(50);

    // Print the top element of the priority queue
    cout << "Top element: " << pq.top() << endl;

    // Remove and print elements from the priority_queue
    cout << "Elements in priority order: ";
    while(!pq.empty()) {
        cout << pq.top() << " ";
        pq.pop();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this snippet, the priority_queue is initialized with three template parameters: the stored type (int), the container used (vector<int>), and the comparison function (greater<int>). It will then sort the elements in ascending order.

