To remove an element from a C++ vector, you can use the erase() method. This method takes an iterator to the position of the element that you want to remove, and removes that element from the vector.

Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to remove an element from a vector:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> myVec {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

    // Remove the second element from the vector
    myVec.erase(myVec.begin() + 1);

    // Print out the elements of the vector
    for (int element : myVec) {
        std::cout << element << " ";
    }
    std::cout << "\n";

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the second element (with value 2) is removed from the vector using the erase() method. The output of the program shows that the element has been successfully removed from the vector:

```
1 3 4 5
```

You can use erase() method within a for loop to remove elements from a C++ vector. However, there is a caveat to using erase() within a loop: erasing an element from a vector invalidates any iterators pointing to elements after the erased one. This means that you must be careful when iterating over the vector after erasing elements to avoid accessing an invalidated iterator.

One way to use erase() in a for loop is to use a reverse iterator and erase elements from the end of the vector. This allows you to delete elements without invalidating any iterators, since erasing elements from the end does not affect any elements that come earlier in the vector. Here's an example:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> myVec {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

    // Remove all even numbers from the vector
    for (auto it = myVec.rbegin(); it != myVec.rend(); ++it) {
        if (*it % 2 == 0) {
            myVec.erase(std::next(it).base()); // erase() takes an iterator to the element to be removed
        }
    }

    // Print out the elements of the vector
    for (int element : myVec) {
        std::cout << element << " ";
    }
    std::cout << "\n";

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we use a reverse iterator (rbegin() and rend()) to loop through the vector from back to front. We then check if the element is even and, if it is, erase it from the vector using erase().

Note that when calling erase() on a reverse iterator, we need to use std::next(it).base() to convert the reverse iterator to a forward iterator that can be passed to erase(). Finally, we loop through the updated vector and print out the remaining elements.

To remove strings that are not of length l from an array of vectors of strings in C++, you can use the `std::remove_if` algorithm along with a lambda function that checks the length of each string.

Here is an example code snippet that removes all strings from an array of vectors of strings that are not of length l:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <string>

int main()
{ 
    // Example array of vectors of strings
    std::vector<std::string> vec1 = {"abc", "de", "fg", "hijk", "lmn"};
    std::vector<std::string> vec2 = {"opq", "rs", "tuv", "wx", "yz"};
    std::vector<std::vector<std::string>> arr = {vec1, vec2};

    // Remove strings not of length l
    int l = 3; // Length to keep
    for (auto& vec : arr) {
        vec.erase(std::remove_if(vec.begin(), vec.end(), [l](std::string str){
            return str.length() != l;
        }), vec.end());
    }

    // Output updated array of vectors of strings
    for (const auto& vec : arr) {
        for (const auto& str : vec) {
            std::cout << str << " ";
        }
        std::cout << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, the lambda function `[l](std::string str){ return str.length() != l; }` checks whether each string in the vector has length different than l, and the `std::remove_if` algorithm removes all strings that return `true` from this function. The outer loop is used to iterate over all vectors in the array.

