Here's a basic Java program that parses HTML using the JSoup library:

```java
import java.io.IOException;

import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Element;
import org.jsoup.select.Elements;

public class HTMLParser {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "http://www.example.com";
        try {
            Document doc = Jsoup.connect(url).get();
            Elements links = doc.select("a[href]");

            for (Element link : links) {
                String linkHref = link.attr("href");
                String linkText = link.text();
                System.out.println(linkHref + " - " + linkText);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

The program first imports the `Jsoup` library, which provides an easy-to-use API for parsing HTML. It then defines a `main` method that takes a URL (in this case, "http://www.example.com") and uses `Jsoup.connect` to fetch the HTML from that URL. 

The program then uses the `doc.select` method to extract all `a` elements with an `href` attribute. This returns a collection of `Element` objects, which the program iterates over with a for-each loop. 

For each link, the program extracts the `href` attribute and link text using `link.attr("href")` and `link.text()`, respectively. It then prints out the link using `System.out.println`. 

This is a simple example, but JSoup provides a rich set of methods for parsing and manipulating HTML.

