To use an encoder on Arduino, you'll need to wire the encoder to your Arduino board and upload the appropriate code. Here's a simple example of a program that reads the values from a rotary encoder and prints them in the Serial Monitor.

1. Wiring the Encoder:

For this example, let's use a basic rotary encoder that has three pins - A, B, and G (ground). Connect these pins to your Arduino board as follows:

- A (Encoder Pin A) -> Arduino Pin 2
- B (Encoder Pin B) -> Arduino Pin 3
- G (Ground) -> Arduino GND

2. Rotary Encoder Arduino Code:

Upload the following code to your Arduino board:

```cpp
#include <Encoder.h>

const int pinA = 2;  // Pin A of the encoder
const int pinB = 3;  // Pin B of the encoder

// Initialize the Encoder library with the pin assignments
Encoder rotaryEncoder(pinA, pinB);

long oldPosition = 0;  // Store the previous reading of the encoder

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);  // Set up the serial communication
}

void loop() {
  long newPosition = rotaryEncoder.read();  // Read the encoder's position

  // Check if the position has changed since the last read
  if (newPosition != oldPosition) {
    oldPosition = newPosition;  // Update old position to new position

    // Print the new position to the serial monitor
    Serial.println(newPosition);
  }
}
```

Now, when you rotate the encoder and monitor the Serial Monitor, you should see the values change accordingly. This basic example can be further developed for more complex applications, like controlling motors, menu navigation, or adjusting settings in an Arduino project.

