Yes, you are on the right track. It is important to separate and organize the different state machines efficiently. Your idea of having a hierarchy is a good approach. Here is one way to organize them with a hierarchy:

1. Main State Machine: This would be the highest level of state machines, which will mainly determine the overall flow of your game, such as MainMenu, FreeRoam, Battle, etc.

2. UI State Machine: This state machine will be nested under the Main state machine (specifically under FreeRoam, Battle, or other states where UI interaction is required). The UI state machine will handle UI-related states such as navigation, menus, dialogue and other UI elements.

3. CharacterController State Machine: This state machine will also be nested under the Main state machine (specifically under FreeRoam, Battle or other states where player interaction is required). The CharacterController state machine will handle character-related states such as idle, walk, run, attack, etc.

Organizing the state machines this way provides a clear hierarchy and ensures that each state machine is only responsible for its own functionalities. Here's a basic example using Unity's built-in Animator for state machines:

```csharp
public class MainStateMachine : MonoBehaviour
{
    public Animator mainAnimator;

    void Start()
    {
        mainAnimator = GetComponent<Animator>();
    }

    public void EnterBattleState()
    {
        mainAnimator.SetTrigger("ToBattle");
    }

    public void EnterFreeRoamState()
    {
        mainAnimator.SetTrigger("ToFreeRoam");
    }
}

public class UIStateMachine : MonoBehaviour
{
    public Animator uiAnimator;

    void Start()
    {
        uiAnimator = GetComponent<Animator>();
    }

    public void EnterMenuState()
    {
        uiAnimator.SetTrigger("ToMenu");
    }

    public void EnterDialogueState()
    {
        uiAnimator.SetTrigger("ToDialogue");
    }
}

public class CharacterControllerStateMachine : MonoBehaviour
{
    public Animator characterControllerAnimator;

    void Start()
    {
        characterControllerAnimator = GetComponent<Animator>();
    }

    public void EnterIdleState()
    {
        characterControllerAnimator.SetTrigger("ToIdle");
    }

    public void EnterWalkState()
    {
        characterControllerAnimator.SetTrigger("ToWalk");
    }
}
```

In this example, each state machine class will be responsible for managing their respective Animator component with the appropriate states and transitions. The MainStateMachine will handle overall game flow, while UIStateMachine and CharacterControllerStateMachine will handle UI and character interactions, respectively.

