Here is an example code using Puppeteer to get the first, second, and third dt tags from the given HTML:

```javascript
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

(async () => {
  const browser = await puppeteer.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage();

  await page.goto('https://example.com/your-page');

  // Get the first dt tag
  const firstDtTag = await page.$('dl.col-sm-12.col-md-6 > dt:nth-child(1)');

  // Get the second dt tag
  const secondDtTag = await page.$('dl.col-sm-12.col-md-6 > dd:nth-child(2) > a');

  // Get the third dt tag
  const thirdDtTag = await page.$('dl.col-sm-12.col-md-6 > dd:nth-child(3) > a');

  // Log the text content of the tags
  console.log(await page.evaluate(el => el.textContent, firstDtTag));
  console.log(await page.evaluate(el => el.textContent, secondDtTag));
  console.log(await page.evaluate(el => el.textContent, thirdDtTag));

  await browser.close();
})();
```

Note: Replace `https://example.com/your-page` with the actual URL of the page you want to scrape. Also, make sure the Puppeteer is installed and imported in your code.

To get the next element sibling element in Puppeteer, you can make use of the page.$eval() method which allows you to evaluate and extract data from the DOM based on a selector. 

Here is an example of how it can be done:

```javascript
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

(async () => {
  const browser = await puppeteer.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage();

  await page.goto('https://example.com/your-page');

  // Get the element based on a selector
  const element = await page.$('#my-element');

  // Get the next element sibling using the page.$eval() method
  const nextElementSibling = await page.$eval('#my-element + *', el => el);

  await browser.close();
})();
```

In this example, we start by using the page.$() method to get the element we want based on a selector, in this case, the #my-element selector.

Next, we use the page.$eval() method to get the next element sibling of the selected element. Here, we use the CSS selector `#my-element + *` which selects any element that immediately follows the selected element.

Finally, we store the next element sibling element as the `nextElementSibling` variable.

Note that if there is no next sibling element, `page.$eval()` function will return `null`.

