You can detect the end of scrollTo function by using the `onscroll` event and checking if the `scrollTop` property has reached its maximum value (i.e., the end of the scrolling). Here is an example:

```javascript
const element = document.getElementById('my-element');
const scrollEnd = element.scrollHeight - element.clientHeight; // calculate the max scroll value

element.onscroll = function() {
  if (element.scrollTop === scrollEnd) {
    // the scrollTo function has ended, execute something here
    console.log('Scroll ended!');
  }
};

element.scrollTo({ top: scrollEnd, behavior: 'smooth' }); // start the scrollTo function
```

In this example, we attach a callback function to the `onscroll` event of the element we want to scroll. The callback function is executed whenever the user scrolls the element or when the `scrollTo` function changes the scroll position. Inside the callback function, we check if the `scrollTop` property has reached its maximum value (`scrollEnd`). If so, we know that the scrollTo function has ended, and we can execute something else.

In that case, you can use the `scroll` event to detect when the scrolling has stopped. You can create a promise that resolves when the `scroll` event is triggered with the `isScrolling` flag set to `false`. Here's an example:

```javascript
function scrollTo(element, to, duration) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    const start = element.scrollTop;
    const change = to - start;
    const increment = 20;
    let currentTime = 0;

    function animateScroll() {
      currentTime += increment;
      const val = Math.easeInOutQuad(currentTime, start, change, duration);
      element.scrollTop = val;
      if (currentTime < duration) {
        setTimeout(animateScroll, increment);
      } else {
        resolve();
      }
    }

    animateScroll();

    element.addEventListener('scroll', function handleScroll() {
      if (!this.isScrolling) {
        this.removeEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);
        resolve();
      }
    });
  });
}

// usage
scrollTo(document.documentElement, 500, 1000).then(() => {
  console.log('Scrolling has ended!');
});

// easing function
Math.easeInOutQuad = function (t, b, c, d) {
  t /= d / 2;
  if (t < 1) return (c / 2) * t * t + b;
  t--;
  return (-c / 2) * (t * (t - 2) - 1) + b;
};
```

In this example, the `scrollTo` function returns a promise that resolves when the scrolling has ended. The function uses an easing function to animate the scrolling from the current position to the target position. It also adds a `scroll` event listener to the element to detect when the scrolling has stopped. The `isScrolling` flag is set to `true` when the `scroll` event is triggered and set to `false` when the scrolling has stopped. Once the `isScrolling` flag is set to `false`, the `scroll` event listener is removed, and the promise is resolved.

