To see if a composable is recomposing, you can add a print statement or a log message with a unique identifier inside the composable. This way you can track when the composable is recomposing by checking the log messages.

For example, you can add a log message like this inside your composable:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyComposable() {
    Log.d("MyComposable", "Recomposing")
    // Your composable code here
}
```

Then, whenever this composable is being recomposed, you will see a log message printed in the logcat window with the tag "MyComposable" and the message "Recomposing". You can then use this log message to determine when a composable is being recomposed and troubleshoot any issues you might be having with recomposition.

If you've confirmed that the composable is indeed recomposing when the relevant state changes, one possible reason why the background color of the Box is not changing could be that the reference to the `Color.Red` that you're using in the `background` modifier of the Box is not being updated even though the state is changing.

To fix this, you could try using a mutable `Color` object instead and update the value of that color object based on the state:

```kotlin
@Composable
