Converting inheritance and the prototype chain to ES6 class syntax involves the use of `extends` and `super` keywords.

Here is an example of inheritance and the prototype chain using the old prototype-based approach:

```javascript
function Person(name, age) {
  this.name = name;
  this.age = age;
}

Person.prototype.introduce = function () {
  console.log(`My name is ${this.name} and I am ${this.age} years old.`);
};

function Employee(name, age, position) {
  Person.call(this, name, age);
  this.position = position;
}

Employee.prototype = Object.create(Person.prototype);
Employee.prototype.constructor = Employee;

Employee.prototype.describePosition = function () {
  console.log(`I am currently working as a ${this.position}.`);
};
```

Now let's convert this to ES6 class syntax with inheritance:

```javascript
class Person {
  constructor(name, age) {
    this.name = name;
    this.age = age;
  }

  introduce() {
    console.log(`My name is ${this.name} and I am ${this.age} years old.`);
  }
}

class Employee extends Person {
  constructor(name, age, position) {
    super(name, age);
    this.position = position;
  }

  describePosition() {
    console.log(`I am currently working as a ${this.position}.`);
  }
}
```

In this example:

1. We created a `Person` class with constructor and `introduce` method.
2. We created an `Employee` class that `extends` the `Person` class.
3. In the `Employee` class constructor, we called the `super` function with the required parameters to initialize the parent `Person` class members.
4. We also added the `describePosition` method to the `Employee` class.

Now the `Employee` class inherits the `introduce` method from the `Person` class and has its own `describePosition` method.

