Table per type (TPT) is a database design pattern where each subclass of a class hierarchy is represented by a separate table within the database. In Laravel, you can implement TPT using Laravel Eloquent ORM, which is an ActiveRecord implementation for PHP frameworks.

Here is an example of how to implement TPT in Laravel:

Step 1: Define the base model class with the table properties and relationships

```php
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class Animal extends Model
{
    protected $table = 'animals';
    protected $guarded = ['id'];

    public function keepers()
    {
        return $this->belongsToMany(Keeper::class, 'animals_keepers');
    }
}
```

Step 2: Define the subclass models extending the base model

```php
namespace App\Models;

class Lion extends Animal
{
    protected $table = 'lions';
    protected $guarded = ['id'];

    // Additional properties and methods specific to Lion model

}

class Elephant extends Animal
{
    protected $table = 'elephants';
    protected $guarded = ['id'];

    // Additional properties and methods specific to Elephant model

}
```

Step 3: Define relationships to the base model within each subclass

```php
namespace App\Models;

class Lion extends Animal
{
    // ...

    public function animal()
    {
        return $this->belongsTo(Animal::class);
    }
}

class Elephant extends Animal
{
    // ...

    public function animal()
    {
        return $this->belongsTo(Animal::class);
    }
}
```

Step 4: Create database tables for each subclass using Laravel migrations

```php
use Illuminate\Database\Migrations\Migration;
use Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema;

class CreateLionsTable extends Migration
{
    public function up()
    {
        Schema::create('lions', function (Blueprint $table) {
            $table->id();
            $table->string('name');
            $table->integer('age');
            $table->timestamps();

            $table->unsignedBigInteger('animal_id')->unique();
            $table->foreign('animal_id')->references('id')->on('animals')->onDelete('cascade');
        });
    }

    public function down()
    {
        Schema::dropIfExists('lions');
    }
}

class CreateElephantsTable extends Migration
{
    public function up()
    {
        Schema::create('elephants', function (Blueprint $table) {
            $table->id();
            $table->string('name');
            $table->integer('age');
            $table->timestamps();

            $table->unsignedBigInteger('animal_id')->unique();
            $table->foreign('animal_id')->references('id')->on('animals')->onDelete('cascade');
        });
    }

    public function down()
    {
        Schema::dropIfExists('elephants');
    }
}
```

Step 5: Use the subclass models to interact with the database

```php
// Create a new lion
$lion = new Lion(['name' => 'Simba', 'age' => 3]);
$lion->save();

// Create a new elephant
$elephant = new Elephant(['name' => 'Dumbo', 'age' => 5]);
$elephant->save();

// Retrieve all animals and their keepers
$animals = Animal::with(['keepers'])->get();
```

Note that TPT can result in complex and potentially inefficient database queries, so it's important to carefully consider your application's data access patterns and performance requirements before deciding to use this pattern.

