Server-Sent Events (SSE) is a technology that allows the server to send real-time updates to clients over a single HTTP connection. When using SSE, the client opens a connection with the server, and the server sends new data to the client whenever it becomes available. This is different from WebSockets, where both the client and the server can send bidirectional data on the same connection.

With ExpressJS, you can implement server-sent events using the express-sse library. Here's an example:

1. Install express and express-sse:
```bash
npm install express express-sse
```

2. Create an app.js file with the following code:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const SSE = require('express-sse');

const app = express();
const sse = new SSE();

// Serve the HTML client
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.sendFile(__dirname + '/index.html');
});

// SSE route for clients to subscribe to updates
app.get('/events', sse.init);

// Endpoint for updating data
app.post('/update', (req, res) => {
  let data = {
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    message: 'Data updated',
  };

  // Send updated data to clients
  sse.send(data);

  res.status(204).end();
});

// Start the server
const port = 3000;
app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server listening on port ${port}`);
});
```

3. Create an index.html file with the following content:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Server-Sent Events Example</title>
  <script>
    // Connect to the Server-Sent Events route
    const source = new EventSource('/events');

    // Listen for 'message' events and update the page
    source.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
      const data = JSON.parse(event.data);
      const content = document.createElement('li');
      content.textContent = `${data.timestamp}: ${data.message}`;
      document.querySelector('#list').append(content);
    });
  </script>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Server-Sent Events Example</h1>
  <ul id="list"></ul>
</body>
</html>
```

4. Start the server:

```bash
node app.js
```

5. Open your browser and navigate to `http://localhost:3000`.

This example uses express-sse to create a server-sent events connection. When clients navigate to `/events`, they are served an SSE connection. When the server receives a POST request to `/update`, it sends the updated data to all connected clients. The clients then update the document with the new data.

In this example, I will give you a detailed explanation of how the Server-Sent Events (SSE) work using ExpressJS.

1. First, we install the required dependencies:

```bash
npm install express express-sse
```

`express` is used to create the web server, and `express-sse` is a middleware for facilitating the implementation of server-sent events.

2. Then, we create an `app.js` file and import the necessary modules and create instances:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const SSE = require('express-sse');

const app = express();
const sse = new SSE();
```

`app` is the instance of the Express server, and `sse` is the instance of the express-sse middleware. The express-sse library simplifies handling of server-sent events in Express.

3. Next, we define the routes.

The first route serves the `index.html` file containing the client-side code:

```javascript
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.sendFile(__dirname + '/index.html');
});
```

The second route `/events` is for clients to subscribe to server-sent events. When clients connect to this route, they will receive a stream of server-sent events:

```javascript
app.get('/events', sse.init);
```

`sse.init` is a middleware that handles the incoming request and sets up the SSE connection for the client.

The third route `/update` is an endpoint for simulating data updates. When the server receives a POST request at this endpoint, it pushes the updated data to all connected clients:

```javascript
app.post('/update', (req, res) => {
  let data = {
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    message: 'Data updated',
  };

  // Send updated data to clients
  sse.send(data);

  res.status(204).end();
});
```

`sse.send(data)` sends the data as a server-sent event message to all connected clients.

4. Now, we start the Express server:

```javascript
