Setting up Spark in a Java project involves adding the Spark dependencies to your project's build file, creating a `Spark` instance, and configuring the routes and middleware for your web application using the Spark API.

Here are the general steps for setting up Spark in a Java project:

1. Add Spark dependencies to your project
    * Maven: add the following to your `pom.xml`:
        ```xml
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.sparkjava</groupId>
            <artifactId>spark-core</artifactId>
            <version>2.9.3</version>
        </dependency>
        ```
    * Gradle: add the following to your `build.gradle`:
        ```
        implementation 'com.sparkjava:spark-core:2.9.3'
        ```

2. Create a `Spark` instance in your application entry point (e.g. `Main` class)
    ```java
    import static spark.Spark.*;

    public class Main {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            // Create a Spark instance
            Spark spark = new Spark();
            // Configure your web application's routes and middleware
            // (see below for details)
        }
    }
    ```

3. Configure routes and middleware for your web application using the Spark API
    ```java
    import static spark.Spark.*;

    public class Main {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Spark spark = new Spark();

            // Define a route that returns "Hello, world!" at the root URL
            get("/", (req, res) -> {
                return "Hello, world!";
            });

            // Define a route that uses a URL parameter
            get("/hello/:name", (req, res) -> {
                String name = req.params(":name");
                return "Hello, " + name + "!";
            });

            // Define middleware to log requests and responses
            before((req, res) -> {
                System.out.println(req.requestMethod() + " " + req.url() + " " + res.status());
            });

            after((req, res) -> {
                System.out.println(req.requestMethod() + " " + req.url() + " " + res.status());
            });
        }
    }
    ```

4. Start the Spark instance
    ```java
    spark.init();
    ```

Now your Spark web application is ready to run! You can start the application by running the `main` method of the `Main` class.

Of course, this is just a brief overview of the setup process. To build more complex applications, you will need to learn additional Spark features such as template rendering, database integration, and secure communication. However, these steps should give you a basic idea of how to get started with Spark in Java.

