To find a maximum spanning tree, we can change the `edge_cmp` function to sort edges in decreasing order of weight, and modify the `min_spanning_tree` function to use the modified edge weights (by multiplying them by -1) when computing the minimum spanning tree.

```c
// qsort comparator function to sort edges by weight in decreasing order
int edge_cmp_desc(const void* a, const void* b) {
    return ((edge*)b)->w - ((edge*)a)->w;
}

// Find maximum spanning tree in which nodes of U are leaves
edge* max_spanning_tree(int n, edge* edges, int m, int* U, int k, int* num_edges) {
    // Sort edges by weight in decreasing order
    qsort(edges, m, sizeof(edge), edge_cmp_desc);

    // Modify edge weights to compute minimum spanning tree
    for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
        edges[i].w *= -1;
    }

    // Compute minimum spanning tree using modified edge weights
    edge* T = min_spanning_tree(n, edges, m, U, k, num_edges);

    // Modify edge weights of T to revert to original values
    for (int i = 1; i <= *num_edges; i++) {
        T[i].w *= -1;
    }

    // Return T
    return T;
}
```

Note that the time complexity of both functions is O(|E| log|V|) due to the use of Kruskal's algorithm to compute the minimum/maximum spanning tree.

