A Markov chain is a stochastic process that follows the property of "memorylessness". In the context of numerical sequences, it means that the probability distribution of the next state depends only on the current state, not on the sequence of events that preceded it. An `-order Markov chain indicates that the chain considers the previous ` states to determine the next state.

Here's a simple C++ code that implements a Markov chain for numerical sequences using a transition matrix:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <ctime>
#include <cstdlib>

// Define transition matrix type
typedef std::vector<std::vector<double>> TransitionMatrix;

// Generates a random number with the given probability distribution
int generateRandom(const std::vector<double> &probabilities) {
    double rand_val = (double)rand() / RAND_MAX;
    double cumulative = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < probabilities.size(); ++i) {
        cumulative += probabilities[i];
        if (rand_val < cumulative) {
            return i;
        }
    }
    return probabilities.size() - 1;
}

// Simulates Markov chain for a given number of steps and initial state
std::vector<int> simulateMarkovChain(const TransitionMatrix &transition_matrix,
                                     int steps, int initial_state) {
    std::vector<int> result;
    result.push_back(initial_state);

    for (int i = 1; i < steps; ++i) {
        initial_state = generateRandom(transition_matrix[initial_state]);
        result.push_back(initial_state);
    }

    return result;
}

int main() {

    // Seed random number generator 
    srand(time(nullptr));

    // Define the transition matrix for the first-order Markov chain
    TransitionMatrix transition_matrix = {
        {0.3, 0.7},
        {0.8, 0.2}
    };

    // Number of states in the chain
    int number_of_states = 2;

    // Simulate Markov chain for a given number of steps
    int steps = 10;
    int initial_state = 0;
    std::vector<int> chain = simulateMarkovChain(transition_matrix, steps, initial_state);

    // Print the generated Markov chain
    std::cout << "Generated Markov chain: " << std::endl;
    for (int state : chain) {
        std::cout << state << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, I implemented an S-order Markov chain for numerical sequences, where S=1 (first-order), with a simple transition matrix representing the transition probabilities between two numerical states 0 and 1. The chain starts with an initial state (0 in this case), and the `simulateMarkovChain` function generates a chain of the specified length (`steps`) using the given transition matrix.

You can adapt this code to implement an S-order Markov chain by modifying the transition matrix to work with S-length combinations of states and providing an initial state of the same length. Remember, the higher the order of the chain, the larger the size of the transition matrix will be.

