To allow your code in C# to accept both lower case and upper case inputs as equivalent, you can use the `ToLower()` method or the `ToUpper()` method to convert all the user inputs to either lower case or upper case before comparing them. 

Here's an example of how you can achieve this:

```csharp
string userChoice = Console.ReadLine().ToLower(); // or userChoice = Console.ReadLine().ToUpper();

if (userChoice == "yes")
{
    Console.WriteLine("You chose Yes!");
}
else if (userChoice == "no")
{
    Console.WriteLine("You chose No!");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Invalid input!");
}
```

In this example, the user's input is converted to lower case using the `ToLower()` method. So, if the user inputs "YES" or "YeS", it will be converted to "yes" before being compared to the string "yes", which means, the condition will be true and "You chose Yes!" will be displayed. Similarly, if the user inputs "NO" or "nO", it will be converted to "no" before being compared to the string "no", which means the condition will be true, and "You chose No!" will be displayed.

By converting all inputs to either upper or lower case, your code becomes case insensitive and can accept inputs in any case, making it more user-friendly.

To display a sentence in the center in C#, you need to calculate the width of the console window, subtract the length of the sentence, and divide the result by 2, which will give you the number of spaces needed to center the text horizontally. Here's an example of how you can achieve this:

```csharp
string sentence = "Hello, World!";
int width = Console.WindowWidth;
int spaces = (width - sentence.Length) / 2;

Console.WriteLine("{0}{1}{0}", new string(' ', spaces), sentence);
```

In this example, we first define the sentence as "Hello, World!" and obtain the width of the console window using the `WindowWidth` property of the Console class. We then subtract the length of the sentence from the width and divide the result by 2 to get the number of spaces needed to center the text.

Finally, we use the `WriteLine` method to display the sentence in the center by using string formatting. We create a new string made of spaces using the `new string()` constructor with a space character and the number of spaces previously calculated. We then insert this string before and after the sentence using curly braces `{}` and the `string.Format()` method. The result is the sentence displayed in the center of the console window.

Note that the console font needs to be of a fixed width for this method to work correctly. Otherwise, the sentence may not be exactly centered.

