To achieve this, you'll first need to add the JGit library to your Spring Boot project by including the following dependency in your `pom.xml`:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.eclipse.jgit</groupId>
    <artifactId>org.eclipse.jgit</artifactId>
    <version>5.13.0.202109080827-r</version>
</dependency>
```

Now we can use JGit's API to clone and pull changes from a remote repository. The example below assumes the use of a local directory and a remote repository URL. If you need authentication, you can pass credentials using `CredentialsProvider` as shown in this example.

Create a new class, e.g., `GitRepositoryManager.java`. This class contains a method, `cloneOrPullRepository()`, which will check if the repository exists locally, clone it if it doesn't, and pull any new changes if it does.

```java
import org.eclipse.jgit.api.CloneCommand;
import org.eclipse.jgit.api.Git;
import org.eclipse.jgit.lib.Repository;
import org.eclipse.jgit.storage.file.FileRepositoryBuilder;
import org.eclipse.jgit.transport.UsernamePasswordCredentialsProvider;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;

public class GitRepositoryManager {

    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(GitRepositoryManager.class);

    public void cloneOrPullRepository(String remoteRepoUrl, String localRepoDir, String username, String password) throws IOException {
        File localRepo = new File(localRepoDir);
        Git git;

        if (!localRepo.exists()) {
            logger.info("Local repository not found, cloning remote repository...");
            CloneCommand cloneCommand = Git.cloneRepository();
            cloneCommand.setURI(remoteRepoUrl);
            cloneCommand.setDirectory(localRepo);
            cloneCommand.setCredentialsProvider(new UsernamePasswordCredentialsProvider(username, password));

            try {
                git = cloneCommand.call();
                logger.info("Repository cloned successfully.");
            } catch (Exception e) {
                logger.error("Error cloning repository: ", e);
                return;
            }
        } else {
            logger.info("Local repository found, opening it...");
            try {
                FileRepositoryBuilder repoBuilder = new FileRepositoryBuilder();
                Repository repository = repoBuilder.setGitDir(localRepo)
                        .readEnvironment()
                        .findGitDir()
                        .setMustExist(true)
                        .build();
                git = new Git(repository);
            } catch (IOException e) {
                logger.error("Error opening local repository: ", e);
                return;
            }

            logger.info("Pulling changes from remote repository...");
            try {
                git.pull()
                        .setCredentialsProvider(new UsernamePasswordCredentialsProvider(username, password))
                        .call();
                logger.info("Pull completed successfully.");
            } catch (Exception e) {
                logger.error("Error pulling changes from remote repository: ", e);
            }
        }

        if (git != null) {
            git.close();
        }
    }
}
```

Now you can use the `GitRepositoryManager` class in your Spring Boot project. It's a good idea to use it as a service and call the `cloneOrPullRepository()` method to clone or pull changes from a remote repository. Example usage:

```java
// In your main Spring Boot class, or any appropriate class where you want to clone/pull repo
@Autowired
private GitRepositoryManager gitRepositoryManager;

public void runExamples() {
    String remoteRepositoryUrl = "https://github.com/username/reponame.git";
    String localRepositoryDirectory = "/path/to/local/repo";
    String username = "your-username";
    String password = "your-password";

    try {
        gitRepositoryManager.cloneOrPullRepository(remoteRepositoryUrl, localRepositoryDirectory, username, password);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        logger.error("Error in cloning or pulling repository: ", e);
    }
}
```

