Netcode for GameObjects (formerly known as MLAPI) is a Unity package that allows you to create multiplayer games in Unity and synchronize data between clients. Here are the steps to set up Netcode for GameObjects and synchronize the data between clients:

1. Install Netcode for GameObjects in your Unity project:

   a. In the Unity Editor, click "Window > Package Manager".

   b. In the Package Manager window, click the "+" button in the upper left corner and select "Add package from git URL...".

   c. Enter "https://github.com/Unity-Technologies/com.unity.netcode.gameobjects.git" as the package URL and click "Add".

2. Set up the Network Manager:

   a. In your Unity project, create an empty GameObject and name it "NetworkManager".

   b. Select the "NetworkManager" GameObject and, in the Inspector tab, click "Add Component".

   c. Search for "NetworkManager" and add it.

   d. Add another component called "NetworkManagerHud" which provides a simple GUI for testing purposes.

3. Set up player prefab:

   a. Create a GameObject or use an existing character model as a prefab, and name it "Player".

   b. Select the "Player" prefab and, in the Inspector tab, click "Add Component".

   c. Search for "NetworkObject" and add it.

   d. Add your player movement script/component to the player prefab (write the code according to the game's requirements).

   e. Assign the player prefab to the Network Manager. Select NetworkManager GameObject, and in the Network Manager component, assign the player prefab to the "Player Prefab" field.

4. Set up synchronization of player data:

   a. Open the player movement script you added to the player prefab.

   b. Add the following namespaces at the top of your script:

```csharp
using Unity.Netcode;
using UnityEngine;
```

   c. Make sure that your script inherits from "NetworkBehaviour" instead of "MonoBehaviour":

```csharp
public class YourPlayerScript : NetworkBehaviour
```

   d. Modify your movement code to only run on the local player. Do this by checking "IsLocalPlayer" property from "NetworkBehaviour":

```csharp
if (!IsLocalPlayer)
{
    return;
}
```

   e. Now, to synchronize the data to other clients, create a "NetworkVariable" variable for the data you want to sync:

```csharp
public NetworkVariable<int> yourVariable = new NetworkVariable<int>();
```

   f. Set the initial value of the `NetworkVariable` in the `Start` or `Awake` method:

```csharp
private void Start()
{
    yourVariable.Value = startingValue;
}
```

   g. Update the data inside your movement method, and use the `OnValueChanged` event to synchronize the data:

```csharp
private void Update()
{
    if (!IsLocalPlayer)
    {
        return;
    }

    // Your update logic and modify the NetworkVariable.Value

    yourVariable.Value = newValue;

    // When NetworkVariable.Value changes, this will call SyncDataToClients
    yourVariable.OnValueChanged += SyncDataToClients;
}


private void SyncDataToClients(int oldValue, int newValue)
{
    // This function will be called on all clients when NetworkVariable.Value changes

    // Update data with the new value
}
```

   h. Save your script and go back to Unity.

5. Build and test your multiplayer game:

   a. Save your scene and go to "File > Build Settings".

   b. Add the current scene to the "Scenes In Build" list.

   c. Click "Build And Run" and run the built executable alongside the Unity Editor to test synchronization between multiple clients.

6. Implement other game-related mechanics and data using similar methods and Netcode for GameObjects documentation.

Remember to test your multiplayer game with various platforms, internet connections, and devices to ensure smooth synchronization between clients.

Netcode for GameObjects official documentation can be found at https://docs-multiplayer.unity3d.com/

RPCs (Remote Procedure Calls) are a way to execute a method on the server, a specific client, or all clients. RPCs are useful when you need to update game state, perform an action or relay non-continuously updated data to clients. Here's how you can use RPCs with Netcode for GameObjects in Unity:

1. Make sure you have followed steps 1 to 3 from the previous answer (Installation of Netcode for GameObjects, Network Manager setup, and Player Prefab setup). If you already have everything set up, proceed to step 2.

2. In the script where you want to use RPCs, make sure the following namespaces are included:

```csharp
using Unity.Netcode;
using UnityEngine;
```

3. Make sure your script inherits from `NetworkBehaviour` instead of `MonoBehaviour`:

```csharp
public class YourPlayerScript : NetworkBehaviour
```

4. Define the RPC method in your script. You can have either `ServerRpc` or `ClientRpc`. Make sure the method is annotated with `[ServerRpc]` or `[ClientRpc]`, and has the `Rpc` suffix:

```csharp
// ServerRpc example
[ServerRpc]
public void ExampleServerRpc(int parameter)
{
    // Code to be executed on the server
}

// ClientRpc example
[ClientRpc]
public void ExampleClientRpc(int parameter)
{
    // Code to be executed on all clients
}
```

5. When you want to call an RPC method, use the following method patterns:

```csharp
// For calling a ServerRpc from a client
ExampleServerRpcClient(parameter);

// For calling a ClientRpc from the server
ExampleClientRpcClient(parameter);
```

Here's an example of a script that uses RPCs to update player position:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;
using Unity.Netcode;

public class YourPlayerScript : NetworkBehaviour
{
    private float moveSpeed = 5f;

    private void Update()
    {
        if (IsLocalPlayer)
        {
            float horizontal = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal") * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime;
            float vertical = Input.GetAxis("Vertical") * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime;

            // Update position on the local client
            transform.Translate(horizontal, vertical, 0);

            // Send the ServerRpc to update the position on the server
            UpdatePlayerPositionServerRpc(transform.position);
        }
    }

    [ServerRpc]
    private void UpdatePlayerPositionServerRpc(Vector3 newPosition)
    {
        // Server updates the position and sends a ClientRpc
        // to update the position on all clients
        UpdatePlayerPositionClientRpc(newPosition);
    }

    [ClientRpc]
    private void UpdatePlayerPositionClientRpc(Vector3 newPosition)
    {
        // Update the position on all clients
        transform.position = newPosition;
    }
}
```

In this example, we send a `ServerRpc` to update the player position on the server. The server then broadcasts an `ClientRpc` to all clients, synchronizing the player's position.

Remember to test your multiplayer game with different platforms, internet connections, and devices to ensure smooth synchronization between clients.

For more information on RPCs, consult the official documentation at: https://docs-multiplayer.unity3d.com/docs/concepts/rpcs/index.html

