It seems like the issue is related to setting the `ViewModelStore` after setting the navigation graph. To fix the issue, you can try setting the `ViewModelStore` before setting the navigation graph.

Try changing the order inside `NavigationHost` function like this:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun NavigationHost(
    navController: NavController,
    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,
) {
    val categoriesViewModel = remember { CategoriesViewModel() }
    val categories by categoriesViewModel.categories.collectAsState()

    if (navController is NavHostController) {
        navController.setViewModelStore(ViewModelStore())
    }

    NavHost(navController = navController, startDestination = Screen.Home.route) {
        composable(Screen.Home.route) {
            HomeScreen(categories = categories, navController = navController, modifier = modifier)
        }
        composable(Screen.Favorites.route) {
            FavoritesScreen(categories = categories, navController = navController, modifier = modifier)
        }
    }
}
```

You can also ensure that the `navController` is always a `NavHostController` when calling the `NavigationHost` function:

```kotlin
Scaffold(
    bottomBar = { /* ... */ },
    content = { paddingValues ->
        val navHostController = navController as NavHostController
        NavigationHost(
            navController = navHostController,
            modifier = Modifier.padding(paddingValues)
        )
    }
)
```

Here's a simple example of how to add jumping to a 2D player in Unity using C#:

```csharp
public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    public float jumpVelocity = 10f;
    public float groundCheckRadius = 0.1f;
    public LayerMask groundLayer;

    private Rigidbody2D rb;
    private bool isGrounded;

    void Start()
    {
        rb = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
    }

    void FixedUpdate()
    {
        // Check if player is on the ground by looking for nearby colliders
        isGrounded = Physics2D.OverlapCircle(transform.position, groundCheckRadius, groundLayer);

        // Jump if the player presses the jump button and is on the ground
        if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space) && isGrounded)
        {
            rb.velocity = new Vector2(rb.velocity.x, jumpVelocity);
        }
    }
}
```

This script adds a `jumpVelocity` field that determines how high the player will jump, a `groundCheckRadius` field that determines the size of the circle used to detect nearby colliders, and a `groundLayer` field that determines which layer to look for nearby colliders on.

The `Start` method gets a reference to the player's `Rigidbody2D` component.

The `FixedUpdate` method first checks if the player is on the ground by calling `Physics2D.OverlapCircle` with the player's position, `groundCheckRadius`, and `groundLayer` as parameters. If a collider is found, `isGrounded` is set to `true`. Otherwise, it's set to `false`.

If the player presses the space bar and is on the ground, the player's velocity is set to a new vector with the same x component but a new y component of `jumpVelocity`. This results in the player jumping upward. 

Note: It's important to use `FixedUpdate` instead of `Update` when working with physics in Unity. `FixedUpdate` is called at a fixed rate, while `Update` is called once per frame, which can cause physics inconsistencies.

Here's an example of how to add a dash ability to a 2D player in Unity using C#:

```csharp
public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    public float dashSpeed = 10f;
    public float dashDuration = 0.5f;
    public float dashCooldown = 1f;

    private Rigidbody2D rb;
    private bool isDashing;
    private float dashTimer;
    private float dashCooldownTimer;

    void Start()
    {
        rb = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
    }

    void Update()
    {
        // Check if the player can dash again
        if (dashCooldownTimer > 0)
        {
            dashCooldownTimer -= Time.deltaTime;
        }

        // Dash if the player presses the dash button and can dash again
        if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.LeftShift) && dashCooldownTimer <= 0)
        {
            isDashing = true;
            dashTimer = dashDuration;
            dashCooldownTimer = dashCooldown;

            // Set the player's velocity to the dash speed
            rb.velocity = transform.right * dashSpeed;
        }

        // Stop dashing when the dash timer runs out
        if (isDashing)
        {
            dashTimer -= Time.deltaTime;

            if (dashTimer <= 0)
            {
                isDashing = false;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This script adds a `dashSpeed` field that determines how fast the player will dash, a `dashDuration` field that determines how long the dash will last, and a `dashCooldown` field that determines how long the player must wait before being able to dash again.

The `Start` method gets a reference to the player's `Rigidbody2D` component.

The `Update` method first checks if the player can dash again by subtracting `Time.deltaTime` from `dashCooldownTimer`. If `dashCooldownTimer` is greater than 0, the player can't dash yet.

If the player presses the left shift key and can dash again, the `isDashing` flag is set to `true`, the `dashTimer` is set to `dashDuration`, and the `dashCooldownTimer` is set to `dashCooldown`. The player's velocity is then set to a new vector pointing to the right and with a magnitude of `dashSpeed`, causing the player to dash in that direction.

While the player is dashing, the `dashTimer` is decreased by `Time.deltaTime`. When `dashTimer` reaches 0, the `isDashing` flag is set to `false`, causing the player to stop dashing.

If you want to activate the dash ability only when the player clicks a designated dash button, you can modify the script like this:

```csharp
public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    public float dashSpeed = 10f;
    public float dashDuration = 0.5f;
    public float dashCooldown = 1f;
    public KeyCode dashKey = KeyCode.LeftShift;

    private Rigidbody2D rb;
    private bool isDashing;
    private float dashTimer;
    private float dashCooldownTimer;

    void Start()
    {
        rb = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
    }

    void Update()
    {
        // Check if the player can dash again
        if (dashCooldownTimer > 0)
        {
            dashCooldownTimer -= Time.deltaTime;
        }

        // Dash if the player presses the dash button and can dash again
        if (Input.GetKeyDown(dashKey) && dashCooldownTimer <= 0)
        {
            isDashing = true;
            dashTimer = dashDuration;
            dashCooldownTimer = dashCooldown;

            // Set the player's velocity to the dash speed
            rb.velocity = transform.right * dashSpeed;
        }

        // Stop dashing when the dash timer runs out
        if (isDashing)
        {
            dashTimer -= Time.deltaTime;

            if (dashTimer <= 0)
            {
                isDashing = false;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this modified version of the script, there's a new public `dashKey` field that allows you to specify which key or button the player needs to press to activate the dash. By default, it's set to `KeyCode.LeftShift`.

In the `Update` method, the script checks if the `dashKey` is pressed instead of checking for all keys that are pressed.

