To print out an error message when a SQL Server script encounters an error in a TRY...CATCH block, you can use the RAISERROR function or the PRINT statement.

Here's a sample SQL script using the RAISERROR function:

```sql
BEGIN TRY
    -- Your SQL statement here
    -- Example: Divide by zero error for demo
    SELECT 1 / 0;
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
    DECLARE @ErrorMessage NVARCHAR(4000);
    DECLARE @ErrorSeverity INT;
    DECLARE @ErrorState INT;

    -- Capture error information
    SELECT
        @ErrorMessage = ERROR_MESSAGE(),
        @ErrorSeverity = ERROR_SEVERITY(),
        @ErrorState = ERROR_STATE();

    -- Print error message
    RAISERROR (@ErrorMessage, -- Message text
               @ErrorSeverity, -- Severity
               @ErrorState -- State
               );
END CATCH;
```

Alternatively, you can use the PRINT statement to print the error message:

```sql
BEGIN TRY
    -- Your SQL statement here
    -- Example: Divide by zero error for demo
    SELECT 1 / 0;
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
    DECLARE @ErrorMessage NVARCHAR(4000);

    -- Capture error information
    SELECT
        @ErrorMessage = ERROR_MESSAGE();

    -- Print error message
    PRINT 'Error: ' + @ErrorMessage;
END CATCH;
```

Either way, when an error occurs within the TRY block, the script will go into the CATCH block and print the error message using the RAISERROR or PRINT statements.

